var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"91-developer/#dev_docs","page":"Developer docs","text":"note: Contributing guidelines\nIf you haven't, please read the Contributing guidelines first.\n\nIf you want to make contributions to this package that involves code, then this guide is for you.","title":"Developer documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"91-developer/#First-time-clone","page":"Developer docs","text":"tip: If you have writing rights\nIf you have writing rights, you don't have to fork. Instead, simply clone and skip ahead. Whenever upstream is mentioned, use origin instead.\n\nIf this is the first time you work with this repository, follow the instructions below to clone the repository.\n\nFork this repo\nClone your repo (this will create a git remote called origin)\nAdd this repo as a remote:\ngit remote add upstream https://github.com/dnadlinger/Levels.jl\n\nThis will ensure that you have two remotes in your git: origin and upstream. You will create branches and push to origin, and you will fetch and update your local master branch from upstream.","title":"First time clone"},{"category":"section","location":"91-developer/#Linting-and-formatting","page":"Developer docs","text":"Install a plugin on your editor to use EditorConfig. This will ensure that your editor is configured with important formatting settings.\n\nWe use https://pre-commit.com to run the linters and formatters. In particular, the Julia code is formatted using JuliaFormatter.jl, so please install it globally first:\n\njulia> # Press ]\npkg> activate\npkg> add JuliaFormatter\n\nTo install pre-commit, we recommend using uv as follows:\n\nuv tool install pre-commit\n\nWith pre-commit installed, activate it as a pre-commit hook:\n\npre-commit install\n\nTo run the linting and formatting manually, enter the command below:\n\npre-commit run -a\n\nNow, you can only commit if all the pre-commit tests pass.","title":"Linting and formatting"},{"category":"section","location":"91-developer/#Link-checking-locally","page":"Developer docs","text":"We use lychee for link checking in CI. You can run it locally to avoid waiting for CI. First, install lychee, then run against the repository root using the project config:\n\nlychee --no-progress --config lychee.toml .","title":"Link checking locally"},{"category":"section","location":"91-developer/#Testing","page":"Developer docs","text":"As with most Julia packages, you can just open Julia in the repository folder, activate the environment, and run test:\n\njulia> # press ]\npkg> activate .\npkg> test","title":"Testing"},{"category":"section","location":"91-developer/#Working-on-a-new-issue","page":"Developer docs","text":"We try to keep a linear history in this repo, so it is important to keep your branches up-to-date.\n\nFetch from the remote and fast-forward your local master\ngit fetch upstream\ngit switch master\ngit merge --ff-only upstream/master\nBranch from master to address the issue (see below for naming)\ngit switch -c 42-add-answer-universe\nPush the new local branch to your personal remote repository\ngit push -u origin 42-add-answer-universe\nCreate a pull request to merge your remote branch into the org master.","title":"Working on a new issue"},{"category":"section","location":"91-developer/#Branch-naming","page":"Developer docs","text":"If there is an associated issue, add the issue number.\nIf there is no associated issue, and the changes are small, add a prefix such as \"typo\", \"hotfix\", \"small-refactor\", according to the type of update.\nIf the changes are not small and there is no associated issue, then create the issue first, so we can properly discuss the changes.\nUse dash separated imperative wording related to the issue (e.g., 14-add-tests, 15-fix-model, 16-remove-obsolete-files).","title":"Branch naming"},{"category":"section","location":"91-developer/#Commit-message","page":"Developer docs","text":"Use imperative or present tense, for instance: Add feature or Fix bug.\nHave informative titles.\nWhen necessary, add a body with details.\nIf there are breaking changes, add the information to the commit message.","title":"Commit message"},{"category":"section","location":"91-developer/#Before-creating-a-pull-request","page":"Developer docs","text":"tip: Atomic git commits\nTry to create \"atomic git commits\" (recommended reading: The Utopic Git History).\n\nMake sure the tests pass.\nMake sure the pre-commit tests pass.\nFetch any master updates from upstream and rebase your branch, if necessary:\ngit fetch upstream\ngit rebase upstream/master BRANCH_NAME\nThen you can open a pull request and work with the reviewer to address any issues.","title":"Before creating a pull request"},{"category":"section","location":"91-developer/#Building-and-viewing-the-documentation-locally","page":"Developer docs","text":"Following the latest suggestions, we recommend using LiveServer to build the documentation. Here is how you do it:\n\nRun julia --project=docs to open Julia in the environment of the docs.\nIf this is the first time building the docs\nPress ] to enter pkg mode\nRun pkg> dev . to use the development version of your package\nPress backspace to leave pkg mode\nRun julia> using LiveServer\nRun julia> servedocs()","title":"Building and viewing the documentation locally"},{"category":"section","location":"91-developer/#Making-a-new-release","page":"Developer docs","text":"To create a new release, you can follow these simple steps:\n\nCreate a branch release-x.y.z\nUpdate version in Project.toml\nUpdate the CHANGELOG.md:\nRename the section \"Unreleased\" to \"[x.y.z] - yyyy-mm-dd\" (i.e., version under brackets, dash, and date in ISO format)\nAdd a new section on top of it named \"Unreleased\"\nAdd a new link in the bottom for version \"x.y.z\"\nChange the \"[unreleased]\" link to use the latest version - end of line, vx.y.z ... HEAD.\nCreate a commit \"Release vx.y.z\", push, create a PR, wait for it to pass, merge the PR.\nGo back to main screen and click on the latest commit (link: https://github.com/dnadlinger/Levels.jl/commit/master)\nAt the bottom, write @JuliaRegistrator register\n\nAfter that, you only need to wait and verify:\n\nWait for the bot to comment (should take < 1m) with a link to a PR to the registry\nFollow the link and wait for a comment on the auto-merge\nThe comment should said all is well and auto-merge should occur shortly\nAfter the merge happens, TagBot will trigger and create a new GitHub tag. Check on https://github.com/dnadlinger/Levels.jl/releases\nAfter the release is create, a \"docs\" GitHub action will start for the tag.\nAfter it passes, a deploy action will run.\nAfter that runs, the stable docs should be updated. Check them and look for the version number.","title":"Making a new release"},{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/15-basis/#reference-basis","page":"State bases","text":"An ordered set of Zeeman states drawn from the levels of a species, fixing the index convention for matrix representations of operators: direct indexing gives the state for an index, stateindex the index for a state, and staterange the index block for a whole level.","title":"State bases"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/15-basis/#Levels.StateBasis","page":"State bases","text":"An ordered set of Zeeman states drawn from the levels of a species, fixing the index order for matrix representations of operators.\n\nThe state for a given index is available by direct indexing, and iteration yields the states in index order. The reverse lookups are stateindex for a single state and staterange for the index block of a whole level.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.StateBasis"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/15-basis/#Levels.StateBasis-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:StateSpec}}","page":"State bases","text":"StateBasis(states::AbstractVector{<:StateSpec})\n\nCreates a basis from an explicit list of states, which are indexed in the given order.\n\nLevel specifications given in spectroscopic notation are parsed into their canonical number-spec form (cf. Levels.parse_level); all levels must be of the same kind — fine-structure (NoHyperfineNumberSpec) or hyperfine (HyperfineNumberSpec) — which becomes the type parameter of the basis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.StateBasis"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/15-basis/#Levels.StateBasis-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"State bases","text":"StateBasis(levels::AbstractVector)\nStateBasis(levels...)\n\nCreates a basis from a list of levels, where each level contributes all its Zeeman sublevels (2j + 1, or 2F + 1 for hyperfine levels) in order of increasing m, with the level blocks arranged in the given order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.StateBasis"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/15-basis/#Levels.stateindex-Union{Tuple{L}, Tuple{StateBasis{L}, StateSpec}} where L","page":"State bases","text":"stateindex(basis::StateBasis, state::StateSpec)\nstateindex(basis::StateBasis, level, m)\n\nReturns the index of the given state in the basis.\n\nAn error is raised if the state is not part of the basis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.stateindex"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/15-basis/#Levels.staterange-Tuple{StateBasis, Any}","page":"State bases","text":"staterange(basis::StateBasis, level)\n\nReturns the range of basis indices of the states belonging to the given level.\n\nOn a hyperfine basis, a fine-structure level query returns the range of the whole (l j) manifold, i.e. the union of its F levels.\n\nAn error is raised if the level has no states in the basis, or if its states are not arranged contiguously.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.staterange"},{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/10-states/#reference-states","page":"Levels and states","text":"Types identifying an electronic level within a species, and the parser for spectroscopic notation.\n\nNoHyperfineNumberSpec is the canonical fine-structure form, and HyperfineNumberSpec its hyperfine counterpart carrying an additional F quantum number; SpectroscopicSpec wraps a notation string such as \"D_5/2\" or \"S_1/2 F=4\" (the F= suffix selecting the hyperfine form). Since strings convert to level specifications implicitly, the APIs in the rest of the package accept level strings directly.","title":"Levels and states"},{"category":"constant","location":"95-reference/10-states/#Levels.ORBITAL_SYMBOLS","page":"Levels and states","text":"Orbital angular momentum symbols in spectroscopic notation, in order of increasing l (which is thus the zero-based index into this string).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.ORBITAL_SYMBOLS"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/10-states/#Levels.HyperfineNumberSpec","page":"Levels and states","text":"Specifies a particular hyperfine level of a species with nuclear spin using the relevant low-field quantum numbers.\n\nThe nuclear spin I itself is a property of the species (cf. HyperfineOneElectronSpecies), not the level, so it is not part of the specification; consistency of f with I - j  F  I + j is only checked by the functions that take the species. The total spin quantum number is implicitly assumed to be 12, as for NoHyperfineNumberSpec.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.HyperfineNumberSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/10-states/#Levels.LevelSpec","page":"Levels and states","text":"Specifies a particular electronic level within a species.\n\nThat is, all the quantum numbers to specify a state, except for the projection of the total angular momentum on the quantization axis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.LevelSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/10-states/#Levels.NoHyperfineNumberSpec","page":"Levels and states","text":"Specifies a particular electronic state using the relevant low-field quantum numbers.\n\nThe total spin quantum number is implicitly assumed to be 12 (cf. NoHyperfineOneElectronSpecies).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.NoHyperfineNumberSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/10-states/#Levels.SpectroscopicSpec","page":"Levels and states","text":"Specifies a particular electronic state using spectroscopic notation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.SpectroscopicSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/10-states/#Levels.StateSpec","page":"Levels and states","text":"Specifies a particular electronic state, i.e. a level plus the projection of the total angular momentum on the quantisation axis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.StateSpec"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/10-states/#Levels.StateSpec-Tuple{LevelSpec, Union{Integer, Rational}}","page":"Levels and states","text":"StateSpec(level, m)\n\nCreates a state specification from any level specification and the total angular momentum projection m.\n\nStrings are parsed as spectroscopic notation into the canonical NoHyperfineNumberSpec form, or HyperfineNumberSpec when a hyperfine F= suffix is present (in which case m is the projection m_F).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.StateSpec"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/10-states/#Base.convert-Tuple{Type{HyperfineNumberSpec}, SpectroscopicSpec}","page":"Levels and states","text":"convert(::Type{HyperfineNumberSpec}, s::SpectroscopicSpec)\n\nParses a hyperfine level in spectroscopic notation into its quantum numbers.\n\nThe fine-structure part follows the NoHyperfineNumberSpec grammar, followed by whitespace and the total angular momentum as F= with an integer or fractional value; \"S_1/2 F=4\", \"S_{1/2} F = 4\" and \"D_5/2 F=7//2\" are all accepted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.convert"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/10-states/#Base.convert-Tuple{Type{NoHyperfineNumberSpec}, SpectroscopicSpec}","page":"Levels and states","text":"convert(::Type{NoHyperfineNumberSpec}, s::SpectroscopicSpec)\n\nParses a level in spectroscopic notation into its quantum numbers.\n\nThe orbital angular momentum is given by one of ORBITAL_SYMBOLS, followed by the total angular momentum as a fraction. The underscore separating the two is optional, as are braces around the fraction and the second slash of Julia's // rational literal syntax; \"S_{1/2}\", \"D_5/2\" and \"D5//2\" are all accepted.\n\nAn error is raised if the string carries a hyperfine F= suffix; such levels parse to HyperfineNumberSpec instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.convert"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/10-states/#Base.convert-Union{Tuple{L}, Tuple{Type{StateSpec{L}}, StateSpec}} where L<:LevelSpec","page":"Levels and states","text":"convert(::Type{StateSpec{L}}, s::StateSpec)\n\nConverts the level part of a state specification, e.g. into its canonical StateSpec{NoHyperfineNumberSpec} form from one given in spectroscopic notation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.convert"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/10-states/#Levels.fine_structure-Tuple{Any}","page":"Levels and states","text":"fine_structure(level)\n\nReturns the fine-structure NoHyperfineNumberSpec part of the given level, projecting out the hyperfine F quantum number if present.\n\nThis projection is deliberately not a convert method: a conversion would let a hyperfine state specification silently reinterpret its m_F as an m_J where a no-hyperfine one is expected, whereas an explicit call site documents that only the fine-structure identity is meant.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.fine_structure"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/10-states/#Levels.momentum-Tuple{Any}","page":"Levels and states","text":"momentum(level)\n\nReturns the total angular momentum quantum number of the given level — the one whose projection is the m of a StateSpec: j for a NoHyperfineNumberSpec, F for a HyperfineNumberSpec.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.momentum"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/10-states/#Levels.parse_fine_structure-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"Levels and states","text":"Parses the fine-structure part of a level in spectroscopic notation (an ORBITAL_SYMBOLS letter plus the total angular momentum fraction). orig is the full level string for error messages.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.parse_fine_structure"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/10-states/#Levels.parse_level-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Levels and states","text":"parse_level(level)\n\nParses a level given in spectroscopic notation into its canonical number-spec form — HyperfineNumberSpec if a hyperfine F= suffix is present, NoHyperfineNumberSpec otherwise. Number specs pass through unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.parse_level"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/10-states/#Levels.parse_momentum-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"Levels and states","text":"Parses an angular momentum quantum number given as a fraction (with optional braces and Julia's // tolerated), or, if allow_integer is set, as a plain integer. orig is the full level string for error messages.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.parse_momentum"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/10-states/#Levels.split_hyperfine_suffix-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Levels and states","text":"Splits an optional trailing hyperfine F=<value> suffix (separated by whitespace) off a level string, returning the fine-structure part and the F value string (or nothing if there is no suffix).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.split_hyperfine_suffix"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/10-states/#Levels.state_pairs-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Levels and states","text":"state_pairs(lower_level, upper_level; Δm)\n\nEnumerates the transitions between the Zeeman states of the two given levels, as lower => upper pairs of StateSpecs.\n\nBoth levels must be of the same kind — two fine-structure levels, or two hyperfine F levels (for which the projections are m_F).\n\nΔm is a collection giving the allowed values of the signed projection difference upper.m - lower.m. There is no default, as the appropriate choice depends on the multipole order of the transition and the field geometry; e.g. Δm = -1:1 covers electric-dipole transitions, and Δm = [-2, -1, 1, 2] the eight ⁸⁸Sr⁺ S_12 ↔ D_52 components observed with the Δm = 0 quadrupole amplitude suppressed by geometry.\n\nThe pairs are ordered by increasing lower-state m, then upper-state m; re-sort as needed, e.g. by magnetic-field sensitivity using zeeman_sensitivity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.state_pairs"},{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/20-species/#reference-species","page":"Species and transitions","text":"An atomic species is described by its level energies and the Einstein A coefficients connecting them; transition frequencies, level lifetimes and saturation intensities are derived from those two tables. A species with nuclear spin additionally carries its hyperfine coupling constants, nuclear g-factor and measured electronic g-factors, while the energy and rate tables stay keyed on the fine-structure levels — hyperfine level arguments resolve to those, with the zero-field hyperfine shifts added where the quantity is frequency-like (cf. the hyperfine-structure reference).\n\nFrequencies and rates are in angular units throughout.","title":"Species and transitions"},{"category":"constant","location":"95-reference/20-species/#Levels.BOHR_RADIUS","page":"Species and transitions","text":"Bohr radius a_0 = 4 π ε_0 ħ^2  (m_e e^2).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.BOHR_RADIUS"},{"category":"constant","location":"95-reference/20-species/#Levels.DIPOLE_AU","page":"Species and transitions","text":"Atomic unit of the electric dipole moment, e a_0.\n\nReduced matrix elements are conventionally quoted in these units in the literature; see LevelPolarisability.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.DIPOLE_AU"},{"category":"constant","location":"95-reference/20-species/#Levels.POLARIZABILITY_AU","page":"Species and transitions","text":"Atomic unit of the electric polarisability, e^2 a_0^2  E_h.\n\nPolarisabilities are conventionally quoted in these units in the literature; see LevelPolarisability.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.POLARIZABILITY_AU"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/20-species/#Levels.HyperfineConstants","page":"Species and transitions","text":"Hyperfine coupling constants of one fine-structure level, stored as energies (i.e. h times the conventionally quoted frequency values).\n\nThe magnetic-dipole constant a and electric-quadrupole constant b enter the level Hamiltonian as A  vecI  vecJ plus the standard Casimir quadrupole term (cf. hyperfine_shift); b must be zero unless both I  12 and J  12.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.HyperfineConstants"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/20-species/#Levels.HyperfineConstants-Tuple{}","page":"Species and transitions","text":"HyperfineConstants(; a, b = zero(a))\n\nCreates the hyperfine coupling constants of one level, promoting the two energies to a common type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.HyperfineConstants"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/20-species/#Levels.HyperfineOneElectronSpecies","page":"Species and transitions","text":"Atomic species with only one (relevant) electron – all configurations spin-1/2 – and hyperfine structure from a nuclear spin I.\n\nThe level data (energies, einstein_as, polarisabilities) is keyed on the fine-structure levels exactly as for NoHyperfineOneElectronSpecies — energies are hyperfine centroids, and Einstein A coefficients are fine-structure rates (each hyperfine sublevel decays at the full fine-structure rate, so there are never F-resolved entries). The hyperfine structure itself is described by nuclear_spin, nuclear_g and the per-level hyperfine coupling constants.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.HyperfineOneElectronSpecies"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/20-species/#Levels.LevelPolarisability","page":"Species and transitions","text":"Electric-dipole data for the ac Stark shift (light shift) of one level.\n\nThe shift is evaluated as a sum over the intermediate levels an electric-dipole transition connects to (cf. light_shift). The few channels that dominate are given explicitly by their reduced matrix elements, so that their detuning from the driving laser is accounted for; everything else — the ionic core, and the many weak transitions to high-lying levels — is lumped into a pair of static polarisabilities, which is a good approximation as long as the laser is far detuned from those transitions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.LevelPolarisability"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/20-species/#Levels.LevelPolarisability-Tuple{Any}","page":"Species and transitions","text":"LevelPolarisability(reduced_dipoles; static_scalar, static_tensor)\n\nCreates the light-shift data for one level from a collection of level => reduced matrix element pairs and the static remainder.\n\nLevels may be given in spectroscopic notation, and all quantities are converted to the stored units, so literature values can be written as e.g. 3.078 * Levels.DIPOLE_AU.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.LevelPolarisability"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/20-species/#Levels.NoHyperfineOneElectronSpecies","page":"Species and transitions","text":"Atomic species with only one (relevant) electron – all configurations spin-1/2 – and no hyperfine structure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.NoHyperfineOneElectronSpecies"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/20-species/#Levels.OneElectronSpecies","page":"Species and transitions","text":"Atomic species with only one (relevant) electron, i.e. all configurations spin-1/2.\n\nConcrete subtypes are NoHyperfineOneElectronSpecies and HyperfineOneElectronSpecies; the level-data accessors (Levels.transition_frequency, einstein_a, lifetime, saturation_intensity, level_polarisability) are shared between them, with fine-structure data keyed on the NoHyperfineNumberSpec part of the queried level.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.OneElectronSpecies"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/20-species/#Levels.Species","page":"Species and transitions","text":"Generic type representing an atomic species.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.Species"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/20-species/#Levels.einstein_a-Tuple{OneElectronSpecies, Any, Any}","page":"Species and transitions","text":"einstein_a(species::OneElectronSpecies, lower, upper)\n\nReturns the Einstein A coefficient for the given two levels.\n\nThe sum of all A coefficients for a given upper level is the reciprocal level lifetime, so this is the linewidth contribution in angular units. Hyperfine levels are resolved to their fine-structure part, as A coefficients are fine-structure quantities (the F-resolved rates are branching fractions of them; cf. Levels.hyperfine_reduction).\n\nReturns nothing if there is no decay from upper to lower.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.einstein_a"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/20-species/#Levels.level_energy-Tuple{OneElectronSpecies, NoHyperfineNumberSpec}","page":"Species and transitions","text":"Returns the energy of the given level relative to the species' reference point: the stored (centroid) energy for a fine-structure level, plus the zero-field hyperfine shift for a hyperfine level.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.level_energy"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/20-species/#Levels.level_polarisability-Tuple{OneElectronSpecies, Any}","page":"Species and transitions","text":"level_polarisability(species::OneElectronSpecies, level)\n\nReturns the LevelPolarisability light-shift data for the given level (the fine-structure part for hyperfine levels).\n\nReturns nothing if no such data is known for the level.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.level_polarisability"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/20-species/#Levels.lifetime-Tuple{OneElectronSpecies, Any}","page":"Species and transitions","text":"lifetime(species::OneElectronSpecies, level)\n\nReturns the lifetime of the given level.\n\nThis is the reciprocal of the sum of all the decay rates from the level. Hyperfine levels are resolved to their fine-structure part — every hyperfine sublevel decays at the full fine-structure rate.\n\nReturns nothing if there are no decay channels defined from the given level.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.lifetime"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/20-species/#Levels.saturation_intensity-Tuple{OneElectronSpecies, Any, Any}","page":"Species and transitions","text":"saturation_intensity(species::OneElectronSpecies, lower, upper)\n\nReturns the saturation intensity for the transition between the two levels.\n\nAn error is raised if the two levels are not connected by a known transition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.saturation_intensity"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/20-species/#Levels.transition_frequency-Tuple{OneElectronSpecies, Any, Any}","page":"Species and transitions","text":"transition_frequency(species::OneElectronSpecies, lower, upper)\n\nReturns the frequency of a given transition (in angular units).\n\nFor hyperfine levels (of a HyperfineOneElectronSpecies), the zero-field hyperfine shifts of any F-resolved levels are included on top of the centroid splitting.\n\nAn error is raised if the two levels are not connected by a known transition or if the levels are incorrectly ordered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.transition_frequency"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/20-species/#Levels.validate_hyperfine-Tuple{HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, HyperfineNumberSpec}","page":"Species and transitions","text":"Checks that the F quantum number of the given hyperfine level is compatible with the nuclear spin of the species, returning the level.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.validate_hyperfine"},{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/70-quantum-toolbox/#reference-quantum-toolbox","page":"QuantumToolbox integration","text":"Loading QuantumToolbox.jl activates the LevelsQuantumToolboxExt package extension, which maps Levels objects onto QuantumToolbox.QuantumObjects so that its solvers (mesolve, steadystate_fourier, …) apply directly:\n\nQuantumObject(A, basis::StateBasis) wraps an operator matrix (or a ket coefficient vector) over a StateBasis. Unitful elements of pure time dimension 𝐓^p are stripped in units of µs^p — µs⁻¹ for Hamiltonians in the angular convention, µs^(-1/2) for Lindblad jump operators — so times passed to the solvers are in µs; the reference time scale is configurable via the time_unit keyword.\nbasis(b::StateBasis, state) and projection(b::StateBasis, ket[, bra]) address kets and projectors through Levels.stateindex.\nfourier_hamiltonians decomposes a DrivenTransition into the Hamiltonian Fourier components consumed by QuantumToolbox.steadystate_fourier.\n\nexamples/sr88-ac-zeeman-state-prep is a worked optical-pumping steady-state calculation on top of these conversions.","title":"QuantumToolbox integration"},{"category":"function","location":"95-reference/70-quantum-toolbox/#Levels.PeriodicDynamics.fourier_hamiltonians","page":"QuantumToolbox integration","text":"fourier_hamiltonians(dt::DrivenTransition; δ = 0, time_unit = u\"µs\")\n\nReturns the Fourier components of the rotating-frame Hamiltonian of the model at laser detuning δ, as QuantumToolbox.jl operators: a NamedTuple (; H_0, H_p, H_m, ωd) with\n\nH(t) = H_0 + H_p e^i ω_d t + H_m e^-i ω_d t\n\nwhere H_0 is the static part (frame diagonal, detuning, and the unmodulated laser coupling), H_p = Σ_i X_i e^{i φ_i} / 2 collects the e^i Ω t halves of the harmonic drives, and H_m = H_p^†. The QuantumObject matrices are dimensionless in the angular time_unit⁻¹ convention (µs⁻¹ by default, matching the normalisation of the dynamics layer), with the drive frequency ωd a plain Float64 in the same units (so times passed to the QuantumToolbox solvers are in time_unit). The field order is chosen such that\n\nsteadystate_fourier(fourier_hamiltonians(dt)..., c_ops)\n\ndirectly computes the periodic steady state of the driven master equation with jump operators c_ops.\n\nLaser phase modulation is not representable in a single-harmonic decomposition, so the components correspond to modulation = HarmonicPhaseModulation() (none) in full_hamiltonian.\n\nnote: Note\nThis method is defined in the LevelsQuantumToolboxExt package extension, i.e. only once QuantumToolbox.jl is loaded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.PeriodicDynamics.fourier_hamiltonians"},{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/99-index/#reference-index","page":"Index","text":"Pages = [\n    \"10-states.md\",\n    \"15-basis.md\",\n    \"20-species.md\",\n    \"25-zeeman.md\",\n    \"27-hyperfine.md\",\n    \"30-species-data.md\",\n    \"40-rates.md\",\n    \"45-multipole.md\",\n    \"46-laser.md\",\n    \"47-polarisability.md\",\n    \"50-optics.md\",\n    \"60-periodic-dynamics.md\",\n    \"70-quantum-toolbox.md\",\n]","title":"Index"},{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#reference-periodic-dynamics","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"","title":"Periodic dynamics"},{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Driven-transition-model","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"","title":"Driven-transition model"},{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Dressed-state-engine","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"","title":"Dressed-state engine"},{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#ac-Zeeman-shifts","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"The time-averaged shifts an ac magnetic drive imparts on the states themselves, via second-order Floquet perturbation theory (fast, with per-intermediate-state diagnostics and near-resonance warnings) or the nonperturbative Floquet dressing as its cross-check. For hyperfine species, these operate on the exact Levels.hyperfine_manifold eigenstates.","title":"ac Zeeman shifts"},{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Exact-engine","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"","title":"Exact engine"},{"category":"module","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Levels.PeriodicDynamics","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"Simulation of optical transitions between Zeeman manifolds under periodic driving, e.g. by the rf fields of a Paul trap: ac Zeeman coupling, laser phase modulation from excess micromotion, and the resulting sidebands, shifts, and modifications to the effective Rabi frequencies.\n\nThe model is assembled in the frame rotating at the laser frequency (optical RWA): a DrivenTransition collects the rotating-frame energies for a static magnetic field, any number of harmonic drive terms at the fundamental drive frequency (e.g. an ac magnetic field via zeeman_drive), and the laser coupling matrix, optionally phase-modulated (e.g. HarmonicPhaseModulation for excess micromotion). Two engines evaluate it: fast dressed-state Floquet perturbation theory (dress, sideband_amplitude), and exact monodromy-matrix propagation of the time-dependent Schrödinger equation (exact_sideband, stroboscopic_populations) as a nonperturbative cross-check.\n\nThe ac Zeeman shifts themselves — with or without a probe laser — are available directly through the second-order perturbation sum ac_zeeman_shift (with ac_zeeman_contributions diagnostics and near-resonance warnings) and its nonperturbative counterpart floquet_zeeman_shift; for hyperfine species these operate on the exact Levels.hyperfine_manifold eigenstates, whose F mixing the hyperfine DrivenTransition method likewise accounts for by rotating couplings and drives into the field eigenbasis.\n\nCf. Joshi et al., \"Characterization of ion-trap-induced ac magnetic fields\", Phys. Rev. A 110, 063101 (2024), doi:10.1103/PhysRevA.110.063101.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.PeriodicDynamics"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Levels.PeriodicDynamics.DrivenTransition","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"Rotating-frame (optical RWA) model of a laser-probed transition between the Zeeman states of two levels under periodic driving:\n\nH(t) = mathrmdiag(textttframe) - δ P_mathrmupper\n+ sum_i X_i cos(Ω t + φ_i) + f(t) L + f(t)^* L^\n\nwith f(t) = e^-i φ_mathrmmod(t)  2 the laser phase-modulation factor. The laser detuning δ from the probed transition and the phase modulation φ_mathrmmod are supplied at evaluation time (cf. full_hamiltonian, sideband_amplitude), so one model instance can serve e.g. a whole modulation-index scan.\n\nConstructed via the DrivenTransition(species, basis, probe; ...) method; all fields are in angular frequency units.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.PeriodicDynamics.DrivenTransition"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Levels.PeriodicDynamics.DrivenTransition-Tuple{NoHyperfineOneElectronSpecies, StateBasis{NoHyperfineNumberSpec}, Pair}","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"DrivenTransition(species, basis, probe::Pair; drive_frequency, static_field,\n                 coupling, drives = HarmonicDrive[])\n\nAssembles the rotating-frame model for probing the lower => upper pair of Zeeman states probe.\n\nThe rotating-frame energies are the Zeeman shifts in static_field (the static magnetic flux density along the quantisation axis ẑ) relative to the probed pair. coupling is the complex matrix of laser carrier Rabi frequencies (angular units) in the upper⟨row|lower⟩⟨col| block, e.g. from Levels.rabi_normalised for a beam geometry, or built directly for couplings only present via mediator levels. drives are the harmonic terms at drive_frequency, e.g. from zeeman_drive.\n\nThe basis must consist of exactly the two probed levels; the harmonic drives must be block-diagonal in them.\n\nFor a HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, the basis must consist of the two complete fine-structure manifolds the probed hyperfine states belong to (all F, all m_F, contiguous per manifold — e.g. from StateBasis(species, \"S_1/2\", \"D_5/2\")). The basis states then denote the adiabatically-labelled eigenstates of the hyperfine + Zeeman Hamiltonian at static_field (cf. Levels.hyperfine_manifold): the rotating-frame energies are the exact manifold eigen-energies, and coupling and drives — supplied in the coupled zero-field F m_F basis, e.g. from Levels.quadrupole_couplings and zeeman_drives — are rotated into that eigenbasis internally via Levels.eigenbasis_transform, which accounts exactly for the F mixing at the working field. Always supply the zero-field coupled-basis matrices: a matrix already rotated to the eigenbasis (e.g. with eigenbasis_transform by hand) would be rotated twice.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.PeriodicDynamics.DrivenTransition"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Levels.PeriodicDynamics.HarmonicDrive","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"A Hermitian drive term X cos(Ω t + φ) at the fundamental drive frequency Ω of a DrivenTransition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.PeriodicDynamics.HarmonicDrive"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Levels.PeriodicDynamics.HarmonicPhaseModulation","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"Sinusoidal phase modulation φ(t) = β_mathrmcos cos(Ω t) + β_mathrmsin sin(Ω t) of the laser coupling of a DrivenTransition at its fundamental drive frequency.\n\nFor excess micromotion at the trap rf frequency, β_cos is the in-phase and β_sin the quadrature modulation index.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.PeriodicDynamics.HarmonicPhaseModulation"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Levels.PeriodicDynamics.full_hamiltonian-Tuple{Levels.PeriodicDynamics.DrivenTransition, Any}","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"full_hamiltonian(dt::DrivenTransition, t; δ = 0, modulation = none)\n\nReturns the full rotating-frame Hamiltonian matrix H(t) (in angular frequency units) at the given (unitful) time, for laser detuning δ from the probed transition and the given laser phase modulation (a HarmonicPhaseModulation, or any callable mapping the unitful time to the phase in radians).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.PeriodicDynamics.full_hamiltonian"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Levels.PeriodicDynamics.phase_function-Tuple{Levels.PeriodicDynamics.HarmonicPhaseModulation, Any}","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"Returns a plain function mapping the time in seconds (Float64) to the laser phase modulation in radians, for use inside the numeric engines.\n\nmodulation is either a HarmonicPhaseModulation, or any callable taking the (unitful) time to the phase; Ω is the fundamental drive frequency of the DrivenTransition in angular units.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.PeriodicDynamics.phase_function"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Levels.PeriodicDynamics.zeeman_drive-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"zeeman_drive(species, basis, B; phase = 0.0)\n\nReturns the HarmonicDrive describing an ac magnetic field vecB cos(Ω t + φ) with the Cartesian amplitude vector B, via Levels.zeeman_hamiltonian.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.PeriodicDynamics.zeeman_drive"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Levels.PeriodicDynamics.zeeman_drives-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"zeeman_drives(species, basis, b; phase = 0.0)\n\nReturns the HarmonicDrives describing an ac magnetic field given as a complex Cartesian amplitude phasor b, vecB(t) = mathrmRevecb  e^-i (Ω t + φ): one drive mathrmRe(vecb) cos(Ω t + φ) plus, for genuinely complex b, a second mathrmIm(vecb) cos(Ω t + φ - π2) (identically zero components are dropped). A real b is a linearly polarised field; e.g. vecb = B_0sqrt2  (1 -i 0) rotates in the x–y plane.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.PeriodicDynamics.zeeman_drives"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Levels.PeriodicDynamics.DressedTransition","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"A DrivenTransition with both manifolds dressed by its harmonic drives (laser off), cached so that repeated sideband_amplitude evaluations (e.g. a modulation-index scan) reuse the diagonalisation.\n\nThe quasienergies ε_lower/ε_upper and periodic Floquet modes u_lower/u_upper follow the conventions of dress_manifold.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.PeriodicDynamics.DressedTransition"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Levels.PeriodicDynamics.dress-Tuple{Levels.PeriodicDynamics.DrivenTransition}","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"dress(dt::DrivenTransition; nharm = 8, ngrid = 256)\n\nDresses the two manifolds of the given model by its harmonic drives, returning a DressedTransition.\n\nnharm is the Floquet harmonic truncation of the dressing; ngrid the number of uniform time samples per drive period used for the Fourier analysis of the laser coupling in sideband_amplitude.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.PeriodicDynamics.dress"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Levels.PeriodicDynamics.dress_manifold-Tuple{LinearAlgebra.Diagonal, AbstractMatrix, Any, Any, Any}","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"dress_manifold(H0::Diagonal, H1::AbstractMatrix, Ω, nharm, ngrid;\n               min_overlap = 0.0) -> (ε, U)\n\nComputes the Floquet modes of one manifold driven by H(t) = H_0 + H_1 e^iΩt + H_1^ e^iΩt, truncating the Floquet Hamiltonian at nharm harmonics.\n\nFor each bare level α, the returned mode is the Floquet eigenstate with maximum harmonic-0 weight on α (unambiguous for weak dressing); ε[α] is its quasienergy (in angular frequency units, like H0), and U[:, it, α] the periodic part u_α(t) sampled on ngrid uniform times over one drive period.\n\nA warning is emitted whenever that maximum weight falls below min_overlap (the identification of the dressed state is then ambiguous, e.g. for a near-resonant drive).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.PeriodicDynamics.dress_manifold"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Levels.PeriodicDynamics.manifold_blocks-Tuple{Levels.PeriodicDynamics.DrivenTransition, UnitRange{Int64}}","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"Fourier components (harmonics 0 and +1) of the laser-free Hamiltonian of a DrivenTransition restricted to the manifold covering the index range r, at δ = 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.PeriodicDynamics.manifold_blocks"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Levels.PeriodicDynamics.sideband_amplitude-Tuple{Levels.PeriodicDynamics.DressedTransition}","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"sideband_amplitude(d::DressedTransition; sideband::Int, modulation = none)\n\nComputes the first-order laser coupling between the dressed probed states at harmonic sideband of the drive frequency (+1: probing at ν + Ω, -1: ν − Ω, 0: carrier), with the laser phase-modulated by modulation (a HarmonicPhaseModulation, or any callable mapping the unitful time to the phase in radians).\n\nReturns a NamedTuple with the effective Rabi frequency Ω = 2|g_n|, the resonance detuning δ_res (including the ac shifts of the dressed levels), the complex amplitude amp = g_n (for interference diagnostics), and the carrier Rabi frequency carrier.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.PeriodicDynamics.sideband_amplitude"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Levels.PeriodicDynamics.sideband_rabi-Tuple{Levels.PeriodicDynamics.DrivenTransition}","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"sideband_rabi(dt::DrivenTransition; sideband, modulation = none, nharm = 8, ngrid = 256)\n\nConvenience wrapper: dresses the transition and evaluates sideband_amplitude.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.PeriodicDynamics.sideband_rabi"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Levels.PeriodicDynamics.ac_zeeman_contributions-Tuple{HyperfineManifold, Any, Any, Any}","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"ac_zeeman_contributions(m::HyperfineManifold, state, Ω, drives)\n\nReturns the per-intermediate-state breakdown of the manifold form of ac_zeeman_shift, with each entry carrying the adiabatic StateSpec label of its intermediate state: (state, sideband, coupling, detuning, shift), sorted by decreasing |shift|.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.PeriodicDynamics.ac_zeeman_contributions"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Levels.PeriodicDynamics.ac_zeeman_contributions-Tuple{LinearAlgebra.Diagonal, AbstractMatrix, Any, Integer}","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"ac_zeeman_contributions(H0::Diagonal, H1::AbstractMatrix, Ω, α::Integer)\n\nReturns the per-intermediate-state breakdown of ac_zeeman_shift: a vector of named tuples (k, sideband, coupling, detuning, shift) (angular frequency units), sorted by decreasing |shift|.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.PeriodicDynamics.ac_zeeman_contributions"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Levels.PeriodicDynamics.ac_zeeman_shift-Tuple{HyperfineManifold, Any, Any, Any}","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"ac_zeeman_shift(m::HyperfineManifold, state, Ω, drives; warn_ratio = 20)\n\nReturns the second-order perturbation-theory ac Zeeman shift of the adiabatically-labelled eigenstate state (a StateSpec or basis index) of the hyperfine manifold solution m, for driving at the angular frequency Ω.\n\ndrives is either a vector of HarmonicDrives over the manifold's coupled basis (e.g. from zeeman_drives), or a complex Cartesian field-amplitude phasor vecb with vecB(t) = mathrmRevecb e^-iΩt; either is rotated into the field eigenbasis internally.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.PeriodicDynamics.ac_zeeman_shift"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Levels.PeriodicDynamics.ac_zeeman_shift-Tuple{HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, Pair, Any, Any, Any}","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"ac_zeeman_shift(species::HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, probe::Pair,\n                static_field, Ω, drives; warn_ratio = 20)\n\nReturns the ac Zeeman shift of the transition between the two adiabatically-labelled hyperfine eigenstates probe (lower => upper StateSpecs) at the static field static_field along ẑ, for driving at the angular frequency Ω, as a named tuple (; shift, lower, upper) with shift = upper - lower (positive: the transition frequency increases).\n\ndrives is a complex Cartesian field phasor or a vector of HarmonicDrives over the coupled basis of each manifold (so the phasor form is the natural one here). The two probed states may lie in the same fine-structure manifold (e.g. a microwave qubit) or in two different ones.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.PeriodicDynamics.ac_zeeman_shift"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Levels.PeriodicDynamics.ac_zeeman_shift-Tuple{Levels.PeriodicDynamics.DrivenTransition, Integer}","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"ac_zeeman_shift(dt::DrivenTransition, i::Integer; warn_ratio = 20)\n\nReturns the second-order perturbation-theory ac Zeeman shift of basis state i of the driven-transition model from its harmonic drives, over the manifold containing i (cf. the manifold blocks used by dress).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.PeriodicDynamics.ac_zeeman_shift"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Levels.PeriodicDynamics.ac_zeeman_shift-Tuple{LinearAlgebra.Diagonal, AbstractMatrix, Any, Integer}","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"ac_zeeman_shift(H0::Diagonal, H1::AbstractMatrix, Ω, α::Integer;\n                warn_ratio = 20)\n\nReturns the time-averaged (quasienergy) shift of level α of one manifold driven by H(t) = H_0 + H_1 e^iΩt + H_1^ e^iΩt (the convention of dress_manifold), from second-order Floquet perturbation theory:\n\nδ_α = sum_k  α fracH_1kα^2E_α - E_k - Ω\n    + fracH^_1kα^2E_α - E_k + Ω\n\nAll quantities are in angular frequency units. A warning is emitted whenever a denominator is smaller than warn_ratio times the corresponding coupling matrix element (near-resonant drive, where the perturbative treatment degrades — cross-check such cases with floquet_zeeman_shift).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.PeriodicDynamics.ac_zeeman_shift"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Levels.PeriodicDynamics.floquet_zeeman_shift-Tuple{HyperfineManifold, Any, Any, Any}","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"floquet_zeeman_shift(m::HyperfineManifold, state, Ω, drives; nharm = 8)\n\nReturns the ac Zeeman shift of the adiabatically-labelled eigenstate state of the hyperfine manifold solution m from the nonperturbative Floquet dressing (dress_manifold, truncated at nharm harmonics): the difference between the dressed quasienergy and the static eigen-energy.\n\nExact in the drive amplitude up to the harmonic truncation, so it serves as the cross-check for ac_zeeman_shift near resonances; a warning is emitted when the dressed-state identification becomes ambiguous (harmonic-0 overlap below 0.5).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.PeriodicDynamics.floquet_zeeman_shift"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Levels.PeriodicDynamics.exact_sideband-Tuple{Levels.PeriodicDynamics.DrivenTransition}","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"exact_sideband(dt::DrivenTransition; sideband, modulation = none,\n               bracket_scale = 8.0, nharm = 8, ngrid = 256)\n\nComputes the exact sideband Rabi frequency by minimising the Floquet quasienergy gap over the laser detuning, bracketed around the dressed-state prediction of sideband_amplitude (whose nharm/ngrid settings are passed through).\n\nReturns (Ω, δ_res). This is the gold-standard cross-check for the dressed-state engine, nonperturbative in every coupling, so it also includes all laser-induced shifts; it is several orders of magnitude slower.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.PeriodicDynamics.exact_sideband"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Levels.PeriodicDynamics.floquet_from_monodromy-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"Quasienergies (wrapped into (-Ω2 Ω2, plain angular s^-1) and Floquet eigenvectors at t = 0 from the monodromy matrix U(T) = e^-iεT.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.PeriodicDynamics.floquet_from_monodromy"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Levels.PeriodicDynamics.monodromy-Tuple{Levels.PeriodicDynamics.DrivenTransition}","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"monodromy(dt::DrivenTransition; δ = 0, modulation = none)\n\nComputes the one-drive-period propagator (monodromy matrix) of i dotψ = H(t) ψ at laser detuning δ from the probed transition, with the given laser phase modulation.\n\nReturns the (dimensionless, complex) propagator matrix over the model's basis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.PeriodicDynamics.monodromy"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Levels.PeriodicDynamics.probe_gap-Tuple{Levels.PeriodicDynamics.DrivenTransition, Any, Any}","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"Wrapped quasienergy gap (plain angular s^-1) between the two Floquet states carrying the probed lower- and upper-state characters. At a sideband resonance, the minimum of this gap over the detuning equals the effective Rabi frequency.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.PeriodicDynamics.probe_gap"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Levels.PeriodicDynamics.stripped_hamiltonian-Tuple{Levels.PeriodicDynamics.DrivenTransition, Any, Any}","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"Returns a fast unit-stripped in-place evaluator ham!(H, t) of the full rotating-frame Hamiltonian of the model — the single point where the exact engine leaves unitful quantities (angular s^-1, time in seconds), directly ahead of the ODE solves.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.PeriodicDynamics.stripped_hamiltonian"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/60-periodic-dynamics/#Levels.PeriodicDynamics.stroboscopic_populations-Tuple{Levels.PeriodicDynamics.DrivenTransition, Int64}","page":"Periodic dynamics","text":"stroboscopic_populations(dt::DrivenTransition, num_periods::Int;\n                         δ = 0, modulation = none, initial = dt.lower)\n\nPropagates the model exactly from the basis state with index initial (defaulting to the probed lower state) and returns the populations of all basis states at the stroboscopic times k T for k = 0  num_periods, as a length(basis) × (num_periods + 1) matrix — a direct TDSE Rabi flop for validation. Rows follow the basis order (cf. Levels.stateindex).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.PeriodicDynamics.stroboscopic_populations"},{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/27-hyperfine/#reference-hyperfine","page":"Hyperfine structure","text":"Species with nuclear spin (HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, e.g. ca43) resolve each fine-structure level into its hyperfine F levels (HyperfineNumberSpec, \"S_1/2 F=4\" in spectroscopic notation), whose 2F + 1 sublevels carry the projection m_F.\n\nThe canonical matrix/index convention is the coupled F m_F basis (e.g. StateBasis(species, \"S_1/2\", \"D_5/2\") expands the fine-structure manifolds into all their F levels): the hyperfine interaction is diagonal there (the Casimir shifts of hyperfine_shift), and every matrix is directly indexable through the StateBasis machinery. The m_I m_J product basis appears only as an internal construction device — operators that are natural as Kronecker products (the magnetic-moment components of Levels.moment_operators, nuclear slow / electronic fast, each in order of increasing projection) are conjugated with the Clebsch–Gordan unitary of Levels.coupling_transform (I m_I J m_J  F m_F, Condon–Shortley phases, nuclear spin first).\n\nThe Zeeman convention is H_Z = μ_B B (g_J m_J + g_I m_I), i.e. the nuclear g-factor is stated in Bohr magnetons with the electron-like sign absorbed (μ_I = -g_I I μ_B), and it is the effective moment of the nucleus bound in the ion, not corrected for diamagnetic shielding.\n\nAt working fields the Zeeman interaction competes with the hyperfine intervals (the Breit–Rabi regime — for the ⁴³Ca⁺ D_52 manifold already at a few gauss), so F is mixed and only m_F remains exact. hyperfine_manifold diagonalises one manifold exactly and labels the eigenstates adiabatically: within each exact-m_F block, F labels follow the energy order of the zero-field levels, which is rigorous because levels of equal m_F do not cross as a function of the field. The labels are therefore field-dependent bookkeeping for eigenstates, undefined at B = 0, and zeeman_shift / zeeman_sensitivity for hyperfine states are exact quantities derived from the eigen-solution (the latter via the Hellmann–Feynman theorem), not first-order expressions. insensitive_field finds the roots of the sensitivity, e.g. the ⁴³Ca⁺ clock-transition points at 3.38 G and 4.96 G. eigenbasis_transform exposes the rotation from the coupled basis into the labelled field eigenbasis as a matrix, so any coupled-basis operator — a quadrupole_couplings matrix, a zeeman_hamiltonian of an rf field — follows the eigenstates as V^ X V; per-component transition amplitudes are available directly via transition_amplitude.","title":"Hyperfine structure"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/27-hyperfine/#Levels.HyperfineManifold","page":"Hyperfine structure","text":"Eigen-solution of one fine-structure manifold of a HyperfineOneElectronSpecies at a static magnetic field along ẑ, with the eigenstates carrying adiabatic (F m_F) labels.\n\nm_F is exact (H F_z = 0); F is a nominal label assigned adiabatically — by energy order within each m_F block following the zero-field ordering of the F levels, which is rigorous since levels of equal m_F do not cross as a function of the field. Both energies and the eigenvector columns of states are permuted to align with basis, so stateindex addresses them directly and operators rotate into the eigenbasis as states' * X * states.\n\nConstructed via hyperfine_manifold; requires B  0 (at zero field the labels within each degenerate F level are arbitrary).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.HyperfineManifold"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/27-hyperfine/#Levels.StateBasis-Tuple{HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, Vararg{Any}}","page":"Hyperfine structure","text":"StateBasis(species::HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, levels...)\n\nCreates a hyperfine state basis, expanding any fine-structure level into all its F levels (in order of increasing F, each contributing its 2F + 1 sublevels in order of increasing m_F).\n\nThis is the canonical basis the manifold machinery (hyperfine_manifold) and the periodic-driving models are expressed in.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.StateBasis"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/27-hyperfine/#Levels.coupled_moments-Tuple{HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, Any}","page":"Hyperfine structure","text":"Returns the Cartesian components of the magnetic-moment operator of the given fine-structure manifold in the canonical coupled basis, i.e. Levels.moment_operators conjugated with Levels.coupling_transform.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.coupled_moments"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/27-hyperfine/#Levels.coupling_transform-Tuple{HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, Any}","page":"Hyperfine structure","text":"coupling_transform(species, fs_level)\n\nReturns the Clebsch–Gordan unitary U relating the m_I m_J product basis of the given fine-structure manifold (cf. Levels.moment_operators) to the canonical coupled F m_F basis (cf. StateBasis(species, levels...)): U_(m_I m_J) (F m_F) = I m_I J m_J  F m_F, so a product-basis operator X transforms to the coupled basis as U^ X U.\n\nThe matrix is real (Condon–Shortley phases) and block-diagonal in m_F = m_I + m_J.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.coupling_transform"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/27-hyperfine/#Levels.eigenbasis_transform-Tuple{StateBasis{HyperfineNumberSpec}, Vararg{HyperfineManifold}}","page":"Hyperfine structure","text":"eigenbasis_transform(basis::StateBasis, manifolds::HyperfineManifold...)\neigenbasis_transform(species::HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, basis::StateBasis, B)\n\nReturns the orthogonal matrix V relating the canonical coupled F m_F basis to the field eigenbasis over the given state basis: column k holds the coupled-basis components of the eigenstate carrying the adiabatic (F m_F) label basis[k] (cf. hyperfine_manifold), so a coupled-basis operator X over basis rotates into the eigenbasis as V^ X V. For example, the exact at-field counterpart of a zero-field quadrupole_couplings matrix C is V' * C * V, with the basis states then denoting the adiabatically-labelled eigenstates (individual components are also available directly via transition_amplitude).\n\nThe basis may span any number of fine-structure manifolds, in any state order, but must contain each spanned manifold completely — the eigen-solution lives on the full manifold state space. V is block-diagonal in the manifolds. The species form solves each manifold at the static flux density B (along the quantisation axis ẑ); the manifold form takes pre-solved HyperfineManifolds, which must share one species and field. The column signs follow the eigenvector convention of hyperfine_manifold.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.eigenbasis_transform"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/27-hyperfine/#Levels.hyperfine_level_list-Tuple{HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, Any}","page":"Hyperfine structure","text":"Returns the hyperfine levels a level argument stands for: a single (validated) F level, or all F levels of a fine-structure manifold.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.hyperfine_level_list"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/27-hyperfine/#Levels.hyperfine_levels-Tuple{HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, Any}","page":"Hyperfine structure","text":"hyperfine_levels(species, fs_level)\n\nReturns the hyperfine F levels of the given fine-structure level, in order of increasing F from I - J to I + J.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.hyperfine_levels"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/27-hyperfine/#Levels.hyperfine_manifold-Tuple{HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, Any, Any}","page":"Hyperfine structure","text":"hyperfine_manifold(species, fs_level, B) -> HyperfineManifold\n\nDiagonalises manifold_hamiltonian at the static flux density B ≠ 0 along ẑ and assigns the adiabatic (F m_F) labels (cf. HyperfineManifold).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.hyperfine_manifold"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/27-hyperfine/#Levels.hyperfine_shift-Tuple{HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, Any}","page":"Hyperfine structure","text":"hyperfine_shift(species, level)\n\nReturns the zero-field hyperfine shift of the given F level relative to the centroid of its fine-structure level, in angular frequency units.\n\nWith K = F(F+1) - I(I+1) - J(J+1), this is the standard Casimir expression\n\nE = fracA K2\n  + B  fracfrac32 K (K + 1) - 2 I (I + 1) J (J + 1)\n               4 I (2I - 1) J (2J - 1)\n\n(the quadrupole term only for I J  12), with the constants from the species' HyperfineConstants.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.hyperfine_shift"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/27-hyperfine/#Levels.insensitive_field-Tuple{HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, Pair, Any}","page":"Hyperfine structure","text":"insensitive_field(species, transition::Pair, bracket)\n\nReturns the static field (along ẑ) at which the first-order magnetic-field sensitivity of the given lower => upper hyperfine transition vanishes, found by bracketed root finding (ITP) of zeeman_sensitivity over bracket (a 2-tuple of flux densities).\n\nThere is deliberately no default bracket: sensitivity zeros are typically not unique (e.g. the ⁴³Ca⁺ 729 nm clock transition has them at both 3.38 G and 4.96 G), so the bracket chooses which one is meant. An error is raised if the sensitivity does not change sign over the bracket.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.insensitive_field"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/27-hyperfine/#Levels.manifold_hamiltonian-Tuple{HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, Any, Any}","page":"Hyperfine structure","text":"manifold_hamiltonian(species, fs_level, B)\n\nReturns the hyperfine + Zeeman Hamiltonian of one fine-structure manifold at the static flux density B along the quantisation axis ẑ, over the canonical coupled basis (StateBasis(species, fs_level)), in angular frequency units.\n\nThe energy zero is the hyperfine centroid. The hyperfine part is diagonal (cf. hyperfine_shift); the Zeeman part mixes the F levels while staying diagonal in m_F.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.manifold_hamiltonian"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/27-hyperfine/#Levels.manifold_index-Tuple{HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, HyperfineNumberSpec, Any}","page":"Hyperfine structure","text":"Returns the index of the state (level, m) in the canonical coupled basis of its fine-structure manifold (F ascending, each m_F ascending).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.manifold_index"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/27-hyperfine/#Levels.moment_operators-Tuple{HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, Any}","page":"Hyperfine structure","text":"moment_operators(species, fs_level) -> (; x, y, z)\n\nReturns the Cartesian components of the magnetic-moment operator vecM = (μ_B  ħ) (g_J vecJ + g_I vecI) of the given fine-structure manifold — the Zeeman Hamiltonian is vecB  vecM — in the m_I m_J product basis, in angular frequency units per flux density.\n\nThe product basis is ordered with the nuclear projection slow and the electronic one fast (kron(I-space, J-space)), each in order of increasing projection; Levels.coupling_transform maps it to the canonical coupled basis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.moment_operators"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/27-hyperfine/#Levels.state_energy-Tuple{HyperfineManifold, StateSpec}","page":"Hyperfine structure","text":"state_energy(m::HyperfineManifold, state)\nstate_energy(m::HyperfineManifold, level, m_F)\n\nReturns the eigen-energy (relative to the hyperfine centroid, in angular frequency units) of the eigenstate with the given adiabatic (F m_F) label.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.state_energy"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/27-hyperfine/#Levels.state_moment-Tuple{HyperfineManifold, Any}","page":"Hyperfine structure","text":"Returns the field derivative dEdB of the given adiabatically-labelled eigenstate at the manifold's field, via the Hellmann–Feynman theorem: the expectation value of HB = (μ_Bħ)(g_J J_z + g_I I_z) in the eigenstate (exact per m_F block, as both operators commute with F_z).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.state_moment"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/27-hyperfine/#Levels.transition_frequency-Tuple{HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, StateSpec{HyperfineNumberSpec}, StateSpec{HyperfineNumberSpec}, Any}","page":"Hyperfine structure","text":"transition_frequency(species::HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, lower::StateSpec,\n                     upper::StateSpec, B)\n\nReturns the exact frequency of the transition between two adiabatically-labelled hyperfine eigenstates at the static field B along ẑ, from the manifold eigen-energies on top of the centroid splitting (in angular units).\n\nUnlike the level form, the result is the signed difference upper − lower, as within one fine-structure manifold either state may lie higher.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.transition_frequency"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/27-hyperfine/#Levels.zeeman_hamiltonian-Tuple{HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, StateBasis{HyperfineNumberSpec}, Any}","page":"Hyperfine structure","text":"zeeman_hamiltonian(species::HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, basis, B)\n\nReturns the matrix of the Zeeman Hamiltonian vecB  (μ_B  ħ) (g_J vecJ + g_I vecI) over the given hyperfine basis, in angular frequency units.\n\nB is the static Cartesian magnetic-field 3-vector, with the quantisation axis along z; it must be real for the result to be Hermitian. Unlike the no-hyperfine case, the result is not diagonal in the F levels — the field mixes F within each fine-structure manifold (while remaining block-diagonal across manifolds, and diagonal in m_F for a field along z). Any basis ordering or subset of sublevels is supported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.zeeman_hamiltonian"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/27-hyperfine/#Levels.zeeman_sensitivity-Tuple{HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, StateSpec{HyperfineNumberSpec}, StateSpec{HyperfineNumberSpec}, Any}","page":"Hyperfine structure","text":"zeeman_sensitivity(species::HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, lower, upper, B)\nzeeman_sensitivity(species::HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, transition::Pair, B)\n\nReturns the magnetic-field sensitivity χ = d(E_u - E_l)dB of the transition between the two adiabatically-labelled hyperfine eigenstates at the static field B, in angular frequency units per flux density.\n\nEvaluated exactly (Hellmann–Feynman, no numerical differentiation) from the manifold eigenstates, so it is valid in the Breit–Rabi regime; the transition may lie within one fine-structure manifold or connect two.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.zeeman_sensitivity"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/27-hyperfine/#Levels.zeeman_shift-Tuple{HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, StateSpec{HyperfineNumberSpec}, Any}","page":"Hyperfine structure","text":"zeeman_shift(species::HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, state, B)\n\nReturns the Zeeman shift E(B) - E(0) of the adiabatically-labelled hyperfine eigenstate, in angular frequency units.\n\nUnlike the no-hyperfine (first-order) case, this is the exact shift from diagonalising the hyperfine + Zeeman Hamiltonian, and is nonlinear in B in the Breit–Rabi regime.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.zeeman_shift"},{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/30-species-data/#reference-species-data","page":"Atomic data","text":"Level structure data for the supported species — ⁸⁸Sr⁺ (sr88) and ⁴³Ca⁺ (ca43) — with literature citations for every value.","title":"Atomic data"},{"category":"constant","location":"95-reference/30-species-data/#Levels.ca43","page":"Atomic data","text":"⁴³Ca⁺ ion (nuclear spin I = 72).\n\nHyperfine centroids are referenced to the S_12 centroid; all hyperfine A/B constants are entered as stated in the cited measurements (the signs follow from μ_I  0). The electronic g-factors and Einstein A coefficients marked as such are ⁴⁰Ca⁺ measurements; their isotope dependence is far below the quoted uncertainties.\n\nReferences\n\n[AME2020]: M. Wang, W. J. Huang, F. G. Kondev, G. Audi, and S. Naimi, \"The AME 2020 atomic mass evaluation (II). Tables, graphs and references\", Chin. Phys. C 45, 030003 (2021), doi:10.1088/1674-1137/abddaf.\n[Pak2022]: C. Pak, M. J. Schlitters, and S. D. Bergeson, \"Improved ionization potential of calcium using frequency-comb-based Rydberg spectroscopy\", Phys. Rev. A 106, 062818 (2022), doi:10.1103/PhysRevA.106.062818.\n[Kramida2020]: A. Kramida, \"Isotope shifts in neutral and singly-ionized calcium\", At. Data Nucl. Data Tables 133–134, 101322 (2020), doi:10.1016/j.adt.2019.101322.\n[Arbes1994]: F. Arbes, M. Benzing, Th. Gudjons, F. Kurth, and G. Werth, \"Precise determination of the ground state hyperfine structure splitting of ⁴³Ca II\", Z. Phys. D 31, 27 (1994), doi:10.1007/BF01426573.\n[Nortershauser1998]: W. Nörtershäuser et al., \"Isotope shifts and hyperfine structure in the 3d ²DJ → 4p ²PJ transitions in calcium II\", Eur. Phys. J. D 2, 33 (1998), doi:10.1007/s100530050107.\n[Benhelm2007]: J. Benhelm, G. Kirchmair, U. Rapol, T. Körber, C. F. Roos, and R. Blatt, \"Measurement of the hyperfine structure of the S₁/₂–D₅/₂ transition in ⁴³Ca⁺\", Phys. Rev. A 75, 032506 (2007), doi:10.1103/PhysRevA.75.032506; the signs of the D_52 constants per the erratum, Phys. Rev. A 75, 049901 (2007), doi:10.1103/PhysRevA.75.049901.\n[Tommaseo2003]: G. Tommaseo, T. Pfeil, G. Revalde, G. Werth, P. Indelicato, and J. P. Desclaux, \"The g_J-factor in the ground state of Ca⁺\", Eur. Phys. J. D 25, 113 (2003), doi:10.1140/epjd/e2003-00096-6.\n[Chwalla2009]: M. Chwalla et al., \"Absolute frequency measurement of the ⁴⁰Ca⁺ 4s ²S₁/₂ – 3d ²D₅/₂ clock transition\", Phys. Rev. Lett. 102, 023002 (2009), doi:10.1103/PhysRevLett.102.023002.\n[Hanley2021]: R. K. Hanley, D. T. C. Allcock, T. P. Harty, M. A. Sepiol, and D. M. Lucas, \"Precision measurement of the ⁴³Ca⁺ nuclear magnetic moment\", Phys. Rev. A 104, 052804 (2021), doi:10.1103/PhysRevA.104.052804.\n[Shao2017]: H. Shao, Y. Huang, H. Guan, C. Li, T. Shi, and K. Gao, \"Precise determination of the quadrupole transition matrix element of ⁴⁰Ca⁺ via branching-fraction and lifetime measurements\", Phys. Rev. A 95, 053415 (2017), doi:10.1103/PhysRevA.95.053415.\n[Hettrich2015]: M. Hettrich et al., \"Measurement of dipole matrix elements with a single trapped ion\", Phys. Rev. Lett. 115, 143003 (2015), doi:10.1103/PhysRevLett.115.143003.\n[Ramm2013]: M. Ramm, T. Pruttivarasin, M. Kokish, I. Talukdar, and H. Häffner, \"Precision measurement method for branching fractions of excited P₁/₂ states applied to ⁴⁰Ca⁺\", Phys. Rev. Lett. 111, 023004 (2013), doi:10.1103/PhysRevLett.111.023004.\n[Gerritsma2008]: R. Gerritsma, G. Kirchmair, F. Zähringer, J. Benhelm, R. Blatt, and C. F. Roos, \"Precision measurement of the branching fractions of the 4p ²P₃/₂ decay of Ca II\", Eur. Phys. J. D 50, 13 (2008), doi:10.1140/epjd/e2008-00196-9.\n[Meir2020]: Z. Meir, M. Sinhal, M. S. Safronova, and S. Willitsch, \"Combining experiments and relativistic theory for establishing accurate radiative quantities in atoms: The lifetime of the ²P₃/₂ state in ⁴⁰Ca⁺\", Phys. Rev. A 101, 012509 (2020), doi:10.1103/PhysRevA.101.012509.\n[Kreuter2005]: A. Kreuter et al., \"Experimental and theoretical study of the 3d ²D-level lifetimes of ⁴⁰Ca⁺\", Phys. Rev. A 71, 032504 (2005), doi:10.1103/PhysRevA.71.032504.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.ca43"},{"category":"constant","location":"95-reference/30-species-data/#Levels.sr88","page":"Atomic data","text":"⁸⁸Sr⁺ ion.\n\nReferences\n\n[Sansonetti2012]: J. E. Sansonetti, \"Wavelengths, Transition Probabilities, and Energy Levels for the Spectra of Strontium Ions (Sr II through Sr XXXVIII)\", J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data 41, 013102 (2012), doi:10.1063/1.3659413.\n[Likforman2016]: J.-P. Likforman, V. Tugayé, S. Guibal, and L. Guidoni, \"Precision measurement of the branching fractions of the 5p ²P₁/₂ state in ⁸⁸Sr⁺ with a single ion in a microfabricated surface trap\", Phys. Rev. A 93, 052507 (2016), doi:10.1103/PhysRevA.93.052507.\n[AME2020]: M. Wang, W. J. Huang, F. G. Kondev, G. Audi, and S. Naimi, \"The AME 2020 atomic mass evaluation (II). Tables, graphs and references\", Chin. Phys. C 45, 030003 (2021), doi:10.1088/1674-1137/abddaf.\n[Sansonetti2010]: J. E. Sansonetti and G. Nave, \"Wavelengths, Transition Probabilities, and Energy Levels for the Spectrum of Neutral Strontium (Sr I)\", J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data 39, 033103 (2010), doi:10.1063/1.3449176.\n[Jiang2009]: D. Jiang, B. Arora, M. S. Safronova, and C. W. Clark, \"Blackbody-radiation shift in a ⁸⁸Sr⁺ ion optical frequency standard\", J. Phys. B 42, 154020 (2009), doi:10.1088/0953-4075/42/15/154020.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.sr88"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/30-species-data/#Levels.σ_to_energy-Tuple{Any}","page":"Atomic data","text":"Converts a spectroscopic wavenumber (inverse wavelength) to the transition photon energy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.σ_to_energy"},{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/45-multipole/#reference-multipole","page":"Angular momentum and multipole couplings","text":"Spin-j operator matrices and spherical vector components, plus the geometry-dependent electric-dipole and -quadrupole coupling amplitudes between Zeeman states for a given beam direction and polarisation.","title":"Angular momentum and multipole couplings"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/45-multipole/#Levels.jplus_matrix-Tuple{Any}","page":"Angular momentum and multipole couplings","text":"jplus_matrix(j)\n\nReturns the matrix of the raising operator J_+ for spin j, in units of ħ, in the basis of the J_z eigenstates ordered by increasing m.\n\nThe non-zero elements are m + 1J_+m = sqrtj (j + 1) - m (m + 1); the lowering operator J_- is the adjoint.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.jplus_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/45-multipole/#Levels.jx_matrix-Tuple{Any}","page":"Angular momentum and multipole couplings","text":"jx_matrix(j)\n\nReturns the matrix of the angular momentum operator J_x for spin j, in units of ħ, in the basis of the J_z eigenstates ordered by increasing m.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.jx_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/45-multipole/#Levels.jy_matrix-Tuple{Any}","page":"Angular momentum and multipole couplings","text":"jy_matrix(j)\n\nReturns the matrix of the angular momentum operator J_y for spin j, in units of ħ, in the basis of the J_z eigenstates ordered by increasing m.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.jy_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/45-multipole/#Levels.jz_matrix-Tuple{Any}","page":"Angular momentum and multipole couplings","text":"jz_matrix(j)\n\nReturns the matrix of the angular momentum operator J_z for spin j, in units of ħ, in the basis of its eigenstates ordered by increasing projection m = -j  j.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.jz_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/45-multipole/#Levels.spherical_component-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Angular momentum and multipole couplings","text":"spherical_component(v, q)\n\nReturns the spherical component q  -1 0 1 of a Cartesian 3-vector: v_0 = v_z, v_1 = (v_x  i v_y)sqrt2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.spherical_component"},{"category":"constant","location":"95-reference/45-multipole/#Levels.RANK2_CG","page":"Angular momentum and multipole couplings","text":"The Clebsch–Gordan coefficients 1 μ 1 ν  2 (μ + ν) coupling two rank-1 tensors to rank two, indexed by [μ + 2, ν + 2].\n\nThese nine fixed numbers are tabulated rather than requested from WignerSymbols on each use, as quadrupole_geometry sits in the hot path of Rabi-frequency and light-shift evaluations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.RANK2_CG"},{"category":"function","location":"95-reference/45-multipole/#Levels.beam_vectors","page":"Angular momentum and multipole couplings","text":"beam_vectors(φ_k, γ_pol, η_pol = 0.0) -> (n, ε)\n\nReturns the unit propagation direction n and (complex) polarisation vector ε for a beam at angle φ_k to the quantisation axis ẑ, with k in the x–z plane.\n\nγ_pol rotates the polarisation from the in-(k, z)-plane vector towards ŷ; η_pol is the relative phase of the two components (0: linear polarisation, ±π/2 at γ_pol = π/4: circular). The physical field is E(t) = mathrmRe(ε E_0 e^-i ω t), so beam_vectors(0.0, π/4, π/2) gives ε = (x̂ + i ŷ)sqrt2, a σ⁺ beam along the quantisation axis (driving Δm = +1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.beam_vectors"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/45-multipole/#Levels.dipole_geometry-Tuple{Any}","page":"Angular momentum and multipole couplings","text":"dipole_geometry(ε)\n\nReturns the geometric amplitudes d_q of the electric-dipole coupling for the Δm = q transition channels for polarisation ε — the coefficients of the atomic operators r C^(1)_q in ε  r, i.e. d_q = (-1)^q ε_-q in terms of the (unconjugated) spherical components of the polarisation — as a length-3 complex vector indexed by q + 2 for q = -1 0 1.\n\nA σ⁺-polarised beam, ε = (x̂ + i ŷ)sqrt2, thus carries its full weight in the Δm = +1 channel. The components are kept complex so that the relative phases between the Δm channels are preserved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.dipole_geometry"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/45-multipole/#Levels.quadrupole_couplings-Tuple{HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, StateBasis{HyperfineNumberSpec}, Vararg{Any, 4}}","page":"Angular momentum and multipole couplings","text":"quadrupole_couplings(species, basis::StateBasis, lower, upper, ε, n)\n\nSpecies-first form: for a HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, returns the relative F-resolved coupling amplitudes c = F m 2 Δm  F m β^(2)(F  F) Γ_Δm (cf. Levels.hyperfine_reduction) over a hyperfine basis. lower and upper may each be a single hyperfine F level or a fine-structure manifold, in which case all its F levels present in the basis contribute.\n\nThese are zero-field amplitudes; at finite field the F mixing within the manifolds modifies them. The exact at-field matrix over a complete-manifold basis is V' * C * V with V from eigenbasis_transform (the rotation the hyperfine Levels.PeriodicDynamics.DrivenTransition applies internally — so pass this zero-field matrix there, never a pre-rotated one); individual at-field components are available via transition_amplitude.\n\nFor a NoHyperfineOneElectronSpecies this simply forwards to the species-less form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.quadrupole_couplings"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/45-multipole/#Levels.quadrupole_couplings-Tuple{StateBasis, Vararg{Any, 4}}","page":"Angular momentum and multipole couplings","text":"quadrupole_couplings(basis::StateBasis, lower_level, upper_level, ε, n)\n\nReturns the relative electric-quadrupole coupling amplitudes c_mm = j_l m 2 Δm  j_u m Γ_Δm between the Zeeman states of the two given levels for the given beam geometry (cf. quadrupole_geometry), as a complex matrix over the given basis with entries in the upper⟨row|lower⟩⟨col| block (zero elsewhere).\n\nThe overall scale is arbitrary; it is typically fixed by normalising one probed transition to a known carrier Rabi frequency using rabi_normalised (e.g. one computed from the beam intensity with rabi_frequency).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.quadrupole_couplings"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/45-multipole/#Levels.quadrupole_geometry-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Angular momentum and multipole couplings","text":"quadrupole_geometry(ε, n)\n\nReturns the geometric amplitudes Γ_q of the electric-quadrupole coupling for the Δm = q transition channels for polarisation ε and beam direction n — the coefficients of the atomic operators r^2 (r̂  r̂)^(2)_q in (ε  r)(n  r), i.e. Γ_q = (-1)^q (ε  n)^(2)_-q in terms of the irreducible tensor product of polarisation and beam direction — as a length-5 complex vector indexed by q + 3 for q = -2  2.\n\nA σ⁺-polarised beam along the quantisation axis thus carries its full weight in the Δm = +1 channel. The components are kept complex so that the relative phases between the Δm channels are preserved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.quadrupole_geometry"},{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/40-rates/#reference-rates","page":"Rabi frequencies and coupling strengths","text":"Angular momentum coupling factors between individual Zeeman states, and the resulting Rabi frequencies for a given drive intensity.\n\nFor hyperfine states, the amplitudes relative to the fine-structure reduced matrix element factorise at zero field into an F-basis Clebsch–Gordan coefficient and the 6-j reduction factor of Levels.hyperfine_reduction; the Einstein A coefficients remain fine-structure quantities, of which the squared reduction factors are the branching fractions. At a working field the Zeeman interaction mixes F within each manifold, so transition_amplitude (and rabi_frequency) also take the static flux density as a final argument, evaluating the amplitude exactly between the adiabatically-labelled eigenstates of hyperfine_manifold; whole coupling matrices rotate into the field eigenbasis with eigenbasis_transform.","title":"Rabi frequencies and coupling strengths"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/40-rates/#Levels.clebsch_gordan-Tuple{StateSpec, StateSpec}","page":"Rabi frequencies and coupling strengths","text":"clebsch_gordan(lower::StateSpec, upper::StateSpec)\n\nReturns the bare Clebsch–Gordan coefficient j m R Δm  j m between two fine-structure states, with the rank R the electric-multipole order from multipole_rank.\n\nThis is the purely geometric coupling factor; the general transition amplitude relative to the reduced matrix element — species-aware, covering hyperfine F levels and their exact mixing at a static field — is transition_amplitude.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.clebsch_gordan"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/40-rates/#Levels.hyperfine_reduction-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Rabi frequencies and coupling strengths","text":"hyperfine_reduction(nuclear_spin, lower, upper; rank)\nhyperfine_reduction(species::HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, lower, upper; rank)\n\nReturns the hyperfine reduction factor β^(k)(F  F) relating the amplitude of a rank-k electronic multipole transition between hyperfine levels to that between their fine-structure levels,\n\nβ^(k)(F  F) = (-1)^J + I + F + k\n    sqrt(2F + 1)(2J + 1)\n    beginBmatrix J  J  k  F  F  I endBmatrix\n\nsuch that F mT^k_qF m  (JTJsqrt2J+1) = F m k qF m β^(k). The phase convention matches the coupled basis of Levels.coupling_transform (I m_I J m_J  F m_F, nuclear spin first); in the I  0 limit β  +1 exactly.\n\nThe squares are the relative line strengths: sum_F β^2 = 1 for fixed F (each hyperfine sublevel decays at the full fine-structure rate, with β^2 the branching fractions), which is why F-resolved rates must never be entered as separate Einstein A coefficients.\n\nrank defaults to the electric-multipole order of multipole_rank; pass it explicitly for other operators (e.g. 1 for M1 within one fine-structure level).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.hyperfine_reduction"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/40-rates/#Levels.multipole_rank-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Rabi frequencies and coupling strengths","text":"multipole_rank(lower, upper)\n\nReturns the electric-multipole rank of the transition between the two given levels — 1 (E1) if the level parities differ, 2 (E2) if they are equal — as determined from the orbital angular momenta. Magnetic multipoles and electric orders beyond E2 are not considered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.multipole_rank"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/40-rates/#Levels.rabi_frequency-Tuple{Any, StateSpec, StateSpec, Any, Any, Any}","page":"Rabi frequencies and coupling strengths","text":"rabi_frequency(species, lower::StateSpec, upper::StateSpec, intensity, ε, n)\nrabi_frequency(species, lower::StateSpec, upper::StateSpec, intensity, ε, n, B)\n\nReturns the Rabi frequency (in angular units, such that the excitation probability oscillates as sin^2(Ω t  2) on resonance) for driving the given transition with a running wave of the given intensity, polarisation ε and propagation direction n (cf. beam_vectors; n only enters for quadrupole transitions).\n\nThe coupling strength is obtained from the Einstein A coefficient of the transition following [James1998],\n\nΩ = sqrt6 π c^2 I A  (ħ ω^3)  j m 1 Δm  j m  d_Δm\n\nfor electric-dipole and\n\nΩ = sqrt20 π c^2 I A  (ħ ω^3)  j m 2 Δm  j m  Γ_Δm\n\nfor electric-quadrupole transitions, with d_q and Γ_q the geometric channel amplitudes from dipole_geometry and quadrupole_geometry.\n\nOnly the directions of ε and n matter — both are normalised internally, as the field amplitude is fixed by the intensity.\n\nFor hyperfine states of a HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, the Einstein A coefficient is that of the fine-structure transition, the angular factor is the zero-field transition_amplitude (F-basis Clebsch–Gordan coefficient times the Levels.hyperfine_reduction factor), and the transition frequency includes the zero-field hyperfine shifts. At finite field the F mixing within the manifolds modifies the component amplitudes (at the few-percent level for the ⁴³Ca⁺ D_52 manifold at 0.5 mT); passing the static flux density B (along the quantisation axis ẑ) as the final positional argument evaluates the angular factor exactly at that field, with the states denoting the adiabatically-labelled eigenstates (cf. transition_amplitude). The ω^3 prefactor keeps the zero-field transition frequency, whose Zeeman corrections are fractionally 10^-9 on an optical transition. For a fine-structure species the B form returns the plain result, which is already exact.\n\nReferences\n\n[James1998]: D. F. V. James, \"Quantum dynamics of cold trapped ions with application to quantum computation\", Appl. Phys. B 66, 181 (1998), doi:10.1007/s003400050373; Eqs. (5.11)–(5.13) in the arXiv:quant-ph/9702053 numbering.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.rabi_frequency"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/40-rates/#Levels.rabi_normalised-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, StateBasis, Pair, Any}","page":"Rabi frequencies and coupling strengths","text":"rabi_normalised(couplings, basis::StateBasis, transition::Pair, Ω0)\n\nScales the given relative coupling matrix such that the entry for the lower => upper transition has magnitude Ω0 (its carrier Rabi frequency, in angular units), fixing the physical scale of all the couplings.\n\nAn error is raised if the amplitude for the given transition (nearly) vanishes in couplings, as the normalisation would then be ill-defined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.rabi_normalised"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/40-rates/#Levels.transition_amplitude-Tuple{Any, StateSpec, StateSpec}","page":"Rabi frequencies and coupling strengths","text":"transition_amplitude(species, lower::StateSpec, upper::StateSpec[, B]; rank)\ntransition_amplitude(m_lower::HyperfineManifold, m_upper::HyperfineManifold,\n                     lower::StateSpec, upper::StateSpec; rank)\n\nReturns the amplitude of the rank-rank multipole transition between the two given states relative to the reduced matrix element of the fine-structure transition, mathrmupT^k_qmathrmlo  (JT^kJsqrt2J+1) with q = Δm. rank defaults to the electric-multipole order of multipole_rank; pass it explicitly for other operators (e.g. 1 for M1 within one fine-structure level).\n\nFor fine-structure states this is the bare Clebsch–Gordan coefficient j m k Δm  j m (cf. Levels.clebsch_gordan); for hyperfine states of a HyperfineOneElectronSpecies at zero field it is F m k Δm  F m β^(k)(F  F) with the Levels.hyperfine_reduction factor β, which in the I  0 limit degenerates exactly (including sign) to the fine-structure form.\n\nWith the static flux density B (along the quantisation axis ẑ) as the final positional argument, hyperfine states denote the adiabatically-labelled eigenstates of the hyperfine + Zeeman Hamiltonian (cf. hyperfine_manifold) and the amplitude is exact at that field, including the F mixing within the manifolds (which modifies the ⁴³Ca⁺ D_52 component amplitudes at the few-percent level already at 0.5 mT). m_F stays exact, so each state pair still couples through the single spherical component q = Δm. For a fine-structure species a static field along ẑ leaves the m_J eigenstates unchanged, so B is accepted and ignored — the zero-field form is already exact — keeping generic code uniform across species.\n\nThe manifold-pair form evaluates the same at-field amplitude from pre-solved HyperfineManifolds (which must share one species and field; pass the same manifold twice for a transition within one manifold), avoiding the re-diagonalisation in loops over many components; the matrix counterpart is the eigenbasis_transform rotation. At-field amplitudes connect continuously (including sign) to the zero-field ones at low field, but the individual signs inherit the eigenvector convention of hyperfine_manifold; magnitudes, and relative phases within one consistently-rotated matrix, are the physically meaningful quantities.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.transition_amplitude"},{"category":"section","location":"90-contributing/#contributing","page":"Contributing guidelines","text":"First of all, thanks for the interest!\n\nWe welcome all kinds of contribution, including, but not limited to code, documentation, examples, configuration, issue creating, etc.\n\nBe polite and respectful.","title":"Contributing guidelines"},{"category":"section","location":"90-contributing/#Bug-reports-and-discussions","page":"Contributing guidelines","text":"If you think you found a bug, feel free to open an issue. Focused suggestions and requests can also be opened as issues. Before opening a pull request, start an issue or a discussion on the topic, please.","title":"Bug reports and discussions"},{"category":"section","location":"90-contributing/#Working-on-an-issue","page":"Contributing guidelines","text":"If you found an issue that interests you, comment on that issue what your plans are. If the solution to the issue is clear, you can immediately create a pull request (see below). Otherwise, say what your proposed solution is and wait for a discussion around it.\n\ntip: Tip\nFeel free to ping us after a few days if there are no responses.\n\nIf your solution involves code (or something that requires running the package locally), check the developer documentation. Otherwise, you can use the GitHub interface directly to create your pull request.","title":"Working on an issue"},{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/46-laser/#reference-laser","page":"Laser frequencies","text":"A laser frequency enters the package in one of three forms: a wavelength, an absolute angular frequency, or a RelativeFrequency — an angular offset from the zero-field interval of a named level pair.\n\nThe relative form exists for precision. Tabulated transition energies are uncertain at the tens-of-MHz level, far coarser than the Zeeman and hyperfine splittings the package computes exactly. Anchoring the laser to the line centre lets that coarse tabulated value cancel out of every frequency difference against components of the reference pair — as in the near-resonant channels of light_shift — leaving only the stated offset and exactly-known splittings. Only resolving the laser to an absolute frequency via Levels.photon_energy reintroduces the tabulated value, and with it its uncertainty.\n\nThe reference pair is a frequency marker, not a transition to be driven; for a hyperfine species it names two F levels, whose Zeeman components are degenerate at zero field.","title":"Laser frequencies"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/46-laser/#Levels.RelativeFrequency","page":"Laser frequencies","text":"RelativeFrequency(lower => upper, offset)\n\nA laser frequency given relative to the zero-field lower → upper transition frequency — the reference point at which all Zeeman components of the two levels are degenerate — plus offset (an angular frequency, e.g. 2π * 1.5u\"MHz\").\n\nThe point of the parametrisation is precision: consumers such as the near-resonant machinery of light_shift only ever use offset together with exactly-known Zeeman and hyperfine splittings, so the tabulated transition energy — and its uncertainty, typically tens of MHz, far coarser than the Zeeman structure — drops out. Only converting to an absolute frequency via Levels.photon_energy(species, laser) reintroduces it.\n\nFor a HyperfineOneElectronSpecies the levels must be F levels (\"S_1/2 F=4\"), whose Zeeman components are degenerate at zero field; the reference interval then includes the zero-field hyperfine shifts of the two levels. The reference pair itself need not be a drivable component — it is a frequency marker, not a transition to be driven.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.RelativeFrequency"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/46-laser/#Levels.photon_energy-Tuple{Union{Unitful.Quantity{T, 𝐋, U}, Unitful.Level{L, S, Unitful.Quantity{T, 𝐋, U}} where {L, S}} where {T, U}}","page":"Laser frequencies","text":"photon_energy(laser)\nphoton_energy(species, laser)\n\nReturns the photon energy ħ ω of a laser given by its wavelength, its angular frequency, or a RelativeFrequency; the latter requires the species, as it is resolved against the tabulated transition energies (whose uncertainty — typically tens of MHz — the absolute value therefore inherits).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.photon_energy"},{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/25-zeeman/#reference-zeeman","page":"Zeeman structure","text":"Landé g-factors (LS coupling with the reduced-mass correction to the orbital g-factor, or measured values where a species provides them), first-order Zeeman shifts of individual states, magnetic-field sensitivities of transitions, and the Zeeman Hamiltonian for an arbitrary field direction.\n\nFor hyperfine species the same functions are exact rather than first-order quantities, evaluated from the manifold eigen-solutions of the hyperfine-structure machinery; lande_g of an F level returns the low-field g_F.\n\nFrequencies are angular throughout.","title":"Zeeman structure"},{"category":"constant","location":"95-reference/25-zeeman/#Levels.BOHR_MAGNETON","page":"Zeeman structure","text":"The Bohr magneton μ_B = e ħ  (2 m_e), from the physical constants built into Unitful.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.BOHR_MAGNETON"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/25-zeeman/#Levels.lande_g-Tuple{NoHyperfineOneElectronSpecies, Any}","page":"Zeeman structure","text":"lande_g(species, level)\n\nReturns the Landé g-factor of the given level.\n\nFor a fine-structure level this is g_J — a measured value where the species provides one (lande_g_overrides of a HyperfineOneElectronSpecies), the LS-coupling expression of Levels.ls_lande_g otherwise. For a hyperfine F level it is the low-field g_F,\n\ng_F = g_J fracF(F+1) + J(J+1) - I(I+1)2 F (F+1)\n    + g_I fracF(F+1) + I(I+1) - J(J+1)2 F (F+1)\n\nwith g_I in the Bohr-magneton convention of the species; note that at finite field the hyperfine Zeeman effect is nonlinear (cf. zeeman_shift and hyperfine_manifold), so g_F m_F is only the B  0 slope.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.lande_g"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/25-zeeman/#Levels.ls_lande_g-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Zeeman structure","text":"Returns the LS-coupling Landé g-factor for a single electron (s = 12), using the free-electron spin g-factor (CODATA 2022) and the leading-order reduced-mass correction to the orbital g-factor, g_L = 1 - m_eM with M the ion mass (any distinction from the nuclear mass is far beyond the accuracy of this leading-order form).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.ls_lande_g"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/25-zeeman/#Levels.zeeman_hamiltonian-Tuple{NoHyperfineOneElectronSpecies, StateBasis{NoHyperfineNumberSpec}, Any}","page":"Zeeman structure","text":"zeeman_hamiltonian(species, basis::StateBasis, B)\n\nReturns the matrix of the Zeeman Hamiltonian sum_J g_J (μ_B  ħ) vecB  vecJ in the given basis, in angular frequency units.\n\nB is the static Cartesian magnetic-field 3-vector, with the quantisation axis along z; it must be real for the result to be Hermitian. The result is block-diagonal in the levels of the basis, with matrix elements constructed per-state, so any basis ordering (or subset of Zeeman sublevels) is supported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.zeeman_hamiltonian"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/25-zeeman/#Levels.zeeman_sensitivity-Tuple{NoHyperfineOneElectronSpecies, StateSpec, StateSpec}","page":"Zeeman structure","text":"zeeman_sensitivity(species, lower::StateSpec, upper::StateSpec)\nzeeman_sensitivity(species, transition::Pair)\n\nReturns the first-order magnetic-field sensitivity χ of the transition frequency between the two given states, in angular frequency units per magnetic flux density (e.g. convertible to µs^-1/mT).\n\nThe Pair form accepts a lower => upper pair as produced by state_pairs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.zeeman_sensitivity"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/25-zeeman/#Levels.zeeman_shift-Tuple{NoHyperfineOneElectronSpecies, StateSpec, Any}","page":"Zeeman structure","text":"zeeman_shift(species, state::StateSpec, B)\n\nReturns the first-order Zeeman shift of the given state in a static magnetic field B along the quantisation axis, in angular frequency units.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.zeeman_shift"},{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/50-optics/#reference-optics","page":"Optics utilities","text":"Gaussian beam geometry and the intensity distribution that goes with it, used to turn an optical power into the intensity seen by an ion.\n\nThese functions are generic over the element type, so they work with symbolic expressions as well as unitful quantities.","title":"Optics utilities"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/50-optics/#Levels.GaussBeamParams","page":"Optics utilities","text":"Parameters of a Gaussian beam propagating along the z axis.\n\nGeneric over the type; the code supports both unitful quantities and symbolic expressions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.GaussBeamParams"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/50-optics/#Levels.gauss_intensity-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Optics utilities","text":"gauss_intensity(power, waist, wavelength; r_offset, z_offset)\n\nReturns the intensity at the given point of a Gaussian beam with the specified parameters and total power.\n\nArguments\n\npower: The optical power integrated over the whole beam profile.\nwaist: The 1e^2-radius of the beam at its waist.\nwavelength: The wavelength of the radiation.\nr_offset: If given, the distance of the point from the focus in the radial direction.\nz_offset: If given, the distance of the point from the focus along the direction of propagation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.gauss_intensity"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/50-optics/#Levels.gauss_intensity-Tuple{Any, GaussBeamParams}","page":"Optics utilities","text":"gauss_intensity(power, beam::GaussBeamParams; r_offset, z_pos)\n\nReturns the intensity at the given point of the given Gaussian beam with the specified total power.\n\nArguments\n\npower: The optical power integrated over the whole beam profile.\nbeam: The beam parameters, including the position of the waist along the direction of propagation.\nr_offset: If given, the distance of the point from the beam axis. Defaults to on-axis.\nz_pos: If given, the position of the point along the direction of propagation, in the same frame of reference as beam.waist_pos. Defaults to the waist.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.gauss_intensity"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/50-optics/#Levels.zero_like-Tuple{Any}","page":"Optics utilities","text":"Returns the additive unit (zero) of the same type as the given value, to transparently support unitful and symbolic use cases.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.zero_like"},{"category":"section","location":"#Levels","page":"Levels","text":"Documentation for Levels.","title":"Levels"},{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/47-polarisability/#reference-polarisability","page":"Light shifts and polarisabilities","text":"The ac Stark (light) shift of the Zeeman states of a laser-illuminated ion, composed of two parts: the far-detuned electric-dipole background — the per-state polarisability at the laser frequency, summed over the explicit dipole channels of the level — and the near-resonant channels of one level pair the laser sits close to, resolved down to the individual Zeeman components at the static field.\n\nThe atomic structure is fixed once by LightShiftCoefficients; evaluating a shift for a particular intensity and polarisation afterwards is cheap enough to sit inside the inner loop of a laser-parameter fit. Which levels the background is available for depends on the LevelPolarisability data of the species (for sr88, the S_12 ↔ D_52 clock levels); the channels need only the Einstein A coefficient.\n\nNote that, unlike the electric-quadrupole coupling, an E1 light shift does not depend on the beam direction — only on the polarisation. The two therefore constrain different combinations of the beam parameters.","title":"Light shifts and polarisabilities"},{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/47-polarisability/#Two-observables","page":"Light shifts and polarisabilities","text":"Driving one Zeeman component of a transition also couples the two states involved, off resonantly, to every other component sharing one of them, and the detunings involved are mere Zeeman splittings. driven_light_shift returns the displacement of the driven resonance: the laser is pinned to the probed component — so the laser frequency drops out entirely — and that component itself is excluded as the coherent drive. This is the shift ⁸⁸Sr⁺ clock evaluations quote as the \"E2 ac Stark shift\", and — needing no polarisability data — it covers the 687-nm S_12 → D_32 line just as well.\n\nA beam parked at a stated frequency, resonantly driving nothing, instead shifts every state it illuminates: light_shift with the laser given as a RelativeFrequency — an offset from the zero-field interval of a named level pair — adds the near-resonant channel sum at the exact at-field detunings to the background. The parametrisation is what preserves precision: the tabulated line centre cancels between the laser and the components, leaving only the stated offset and exactly-known Zeeman and hyperfine splittings (see Laser frequencies). This is the regime of a laser placed within the manifold of Zeeman components, poles and all; second-order perturbation theory holds while every channel Rabi frequency stays small against its detuning, and the evaluation warns otherwise. The Ramsey-type shift of a pair from a parked beam is simply the difference of the two states' shifts.\n\nBeing second order in the coupling, the driven shift scales as the intensity over the field, so its size relative to the background is set by the field alone: for ⁸⁸Sr⁺ at 674 nm it is of the same order at 0.5 mT, but up to two orders of magnitude larger at the 4.8 µT of an optical-clock field. What saves the clock is a symmetry rather than a small size — with a linear polarisation the two components of a m Zeeman pair shift equally and oppositely, so the shift cancels in the pair average the servo is steered to, and only the ellipticity leaks through ([Lindvall2025], Sec. III F 2).","title":"Two observables"},{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/47-polarisability/#Electric-dipole-lines","page":"Light shifts and polarisabilities","text":"The channel machinery is rank-agnostic: naming an electric-dipole pair in the RelativeFrequency (e.g. 422 nm S_12 → P_12) moves the co-rotating part of that one polarisability channel out of the background and into the Zeeman-resolved sum — an exact split, with the smooth counter-rotating term staying behind. This is the regime of a beam parked some tens of MHz to a few GHz from a broad dipole line, where tens-of-MHz Zeeman splittings modify the result at a level a Zeeman-blind polarisability cannot see. A bare wavelength that close (~100 GHz) to an explicit dipole channel is refused outright for the same reason.","title":"Electric-dipole lines"},{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/47-polarisability/#Hyperfine-species","page":"Light shifts and polarisabilities","text":"For hyperfine states, the electric-dipole background channels are summed over the hyperfine levels of each intermediate fine-structure level with the F-basis angular factors, and the intermediate splittings are resolved in the detunings — which is what gives e.g. the ⁴³Ca⁺ S_12 F levels a small tensor polarisability their J = 12 parent lacks. The near-resonant channels are built from the eigenbasis-rotated amplitudes and exact intermediate-field eigen-energy differences of the manifolds at the construction field, so the F mixing is accounted for exactly; the reference pair of a RelativeFrequency names two F levels, whose zero-field interval — where the Zeeman components are degenerate — anchors the offset. Note that the m pair cancellation quoted above then survives only approximately: spectators in other F levels sit at hyperfine-interval detunings that are even under m  -m, so their contribution — often the dominant one — does not average out.","title":"Hyperfine species"},{"category":"constant","location":"95-reference/47-polarisability/#Levels.BACKGROUND_GUARD_WINDOW","page":"Light shifts and polarisabilities","text":"A bare laser frequency closer than this to an explicit electric-dipole channel would make the background silently miss the Zeeman/hyperfine structure of the line (which only a RelativeFrequency reference resolves); beyond it, neglecting that structure is a  10^-3 relative error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.BACKGROUND_GUARD_WINDOW"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/47-polarisability/#Levels.LightShiftCoefficients","page":"Light shifts and polarisabilities","text":"Light-shift data for a set of states, precomputed for one laser.\n\nConstruct with LightShiftCoefficients(species, basis, laser[; B]) and evaluate with light_shift (parked beam, single states) or driven_light_shift (resonantly driven component); see there for the sign and unit conventions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.LightShiftCoefficients"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/47-polarisability/#Levels.LightShiftCoefficients-Tuple{Any, StateBasis{NoHyperfineNumberSpec}, Any}","page":"Light shifts and polarisabilities","text":"LightShiftCoefficients(species, basis::StateBasis, laser[; B])\nLightShiftCoefficients(species, levels_or_states::AbstractVector, laser[; B])\n\nPrecomputes the ac Stark shift data of every state in the given basis, for a laser given as a wavelength, an angular frequency, or a RelativeFrequency.\n\nAll the atomic structure enters here, so that evaluating the shift for a particular intensity and polarisation afterwards costs only a handful of arithmetic operations — the intended use when fitting laser parameters against many observed transition frequencies.\n\nTwo kinds of data are held. The far-detuned electric-dipole background — the per-state polarisabilities at the laser frequency — requires LevelPolarisability data; levels without it are admitted, but evaluating their background raises. The near-resonant channels — per-state couplings to the individual Zeeman components of a level pair the laser sits close to, at their exact at-field positions — are built when the static flux density B is given: for every electric-quadrupole pair within the basis (enabling driven_light_shift), and for the reference pair of a RelativeFrequency laser of either rank (additionally enabling the parked single-state light_shift at the laser's stated offset). The channels need only the Einstein A coefficient, not polarisability data.\n\nA RelativeFrequency laser requires B. If its reference pair is an electric-dipole one, the co-rotating part of that channel is moved from the background into the channels (which requires it to be explicit in the LevelPolarisability data rather than lumped into the static remainder). A bare wavelength that falls within ~100 GHz of an explicit electric-dipole channel is refused for the same reason: the background alone would silently miss the Zeeman structure of the line.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.LightShiftCoefficients"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/47-polarisability/#Levels.ResonantChannel","page":"Light shifts and polarisabilities","text":"One near-resonant coupling channel of a basis state: a Zeeman component connecting it to partner in the other manifold of a level pair the laser sits close to.\n\nThe signed weight is such that the second-order shift of the state from this channel is weight × intensity × w / (δ − Δ), with w the geometric weight of its Δm = q component (polarisation_weights[q + 2] for rank 1, quadrupole_weights[q + 3] for rank 2) and δ the laser offset from the same reference Δ is measured against. It is positive for a state in the lower manifold of the pair and negative for one in the upper (level repulsion away from the driving photon); 4 × |weight| × intensity × w is the squared Rabi frequency of the component, and γ the total decay rate of the upper level — both used by the validity warnings at evaluation time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.ResonantChannel"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/47-polarisability/#Levels.dipole_cg-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"Light shifts and polarisabilities","text":"Returns j m 1 q  j m + q, or zero where that is not a valid electric-dipole channel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.dipole_cg"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/47-polarisability/#Levels.driven_light_shift-Tuple{LightShiftCoefficients, Pair, Any, Any}","page":"Light shifts and polarisabilities","text":"driven_light_shift(coefficients::LightShiftCoefficients, transition::Pair, intensity, ε; n, parts)\ndriven_light_shift(species, lower::StateSpec, upper::StateSpec, intensity, ε; n, B, parts)\n\nReturns the light shift of the observed resonance when the lower => upper Zeeman component is resonantly driven (in angular units): the amount by which the frequency at which resonant Rabi flopping is observed exceeds the unperturbed transition frequency, with the laser servo-locked to the probed component.\n\nThe background part is the difference of the two states' far-detuned electric-dipole shifts. The resonant part sums, for both states, the couplings to every other Zeeman component sharing one of them — the driven channel itself is the coherent drive, not a shift — with the laser pinned to the probed component, so the laser frequency drops out and only exact at-field splittings enter the detunings. parts selects :total (the default), :background or :resonant.\n\nThis is the shift ⁸⁸Sr⁺ clock evaluations quote as the \"E2 ac Stark shift\" ([Lindvall2025], Sec. III F 2): with a perfectly linear polarisation the shifts of the two components of a m Zeeman pair are equal and opposite and cancel in the pair average, whereas an elliptical polarisation leaves a net shift. (For a hyperfine species the cancellation survives only approximately — spectators in other F levels sit at hyperfine-interval detunings that are even under m  m; the rigorous mirror identity is shift(−m pair, B) = shift(+m pair, −B).)\n\nThe probed pair may be any electric-quadrupole transition with channels in the coefficients (which requires construction with B); an electric-dipole pair must additionally be the RelativeFrequency reference pair. The beam direction n is required for electric-quadrupole pairs and unused for electric-dipole ones. For the background part, the coefficients' laser frequency must be consistent with the probed transition (rtol 10^-3) — in driven mode the laser is on that line. Like the parked light_shift, the resonant sum is second-order perturbation theory and warns when a spectator Rabi frequency approaches its detuning (which breaks down first for near-degenerate components at very low fields).\n\nReferences\n\n[Lindvall2025]: T. Lindvall, A. E. Wallin, K. J. Hanhijärvi, and T. Fordell, \"⁸⁸Sr⁺ Optical Clock with 7.9 × 10⁻¹⁹ Systematic Uncertainty and Measurement of Its Absolute Frequency\", Phys. Rev. Applied 24, 044082 (2025), doi:10.1103/cztf-bfvp.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.driven_light_shift"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/47-polarisability/#Levels.light_shift-Tuple{LightShiftCoefficients, StateSpec, Any, Any}","page":"Light shifts and polarisabilities","text":"light_shift(coefficients::LightShiftCoefficients, state::StateSpec, intensity, ε; n, δ, parts)\nlight_shift(species, state::StateSpec, laser, intensity, ε; n, B, δ, parts)\n\nReturns the ac Stark (light) shift of the given state (in angular units) for a beam of the given intensity and polarisation ε, parked at the laser frequency the coefficients were built for.\n\nThe shift is the far-detuned electric-dipole background — which depends on the polarisation alone, never on the beam direction — plus, for a RelativeFrequency laser (necessarily built with B), the near-resonant sum over the Zeeman components connecting the state to the other manifold of the reference pair, at their exact at-field detunings from the stated laser offset. For an electric-quadrupole reference pair the beam direction n is required, as the quadrupole coupling depends on it; for an electric-dipole one it is not used. parts selects :total (the default), :background or :resonant.\n\nδ overrides the laser offset (relative to the same reference interval), so a frequency sweep or fit can reuse one set of coefficients.\n\nThe change a parked beam makes to the splitting of a lower => upper pair — a Ramsey-type measurement, with no component resonantly driven — is the difference of the two states' shifts; the displacement of the resonance observed when driving a component is driven_light_shift instead.\n\nThe near-resonant sum is second-order perturbation theory: every channel Rabi frequency must stay small against its detuning, and detunings within a few linewidths of a pole are outside the model; both conditions warn when violated. The one-shot species form is a convenience wrapper that rebuilds the coefficients on every call.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.light_shift"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/47-polarisability/#Levels.polarisability_data-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Light shifts and polarisabilities","text":"Returns the level and its light-shift data, or raises an error if there is none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.polarisability_data"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/47-polarisability/#Levels.polarisation_weights-Tuple{Any}","page":"Light shifts and polarisabilities","text":"polarisation_weights(ε)\n\nReturns the fractions w_q = ε_-q^2 of the beam intensity driving each of the Δm = q electric-dipole channels, as a length-3 tuple indexed by q + 2.\n\nThese are the squared magnitudes of the dipole_geometry amplitudes, normalised to sum to one; only the direction of ε matters, as the overall scale is set by the intensity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.polarisation_weights"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/47-polarisability/#Levels.quadrupole_weights-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Light shifts and polarisabilities","text":"quadrupole_weights(ε, n)\n\nReturns the weights Γ_q^2 with which the beam intensity drives each of the Δm = q electric-quadrupole channels, as a length-5 tuple indexed by q + 3.\n\nThese are the squared magnitudes of the quadrupole_geometry amplitudes for a unit polarisation and beam direction, so only the directions of ε and n matter; the overall scale is set by the intensity. Unlike the electric-dipole polarisation_weights they do not sum to one, as only part of ε  n is of rank two.\n\nThe polarisation must be transverse to the beam direction, ε  n = 0, as it is for any propagating field; pairs that do not belong to one physical beam are rejected.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.quadrupole_weights"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/47-polarisability/#Levels.resonant_channels-Tuple{Any, StateBasis{NoHyperfineNumberSpec}, Any, Any}","page":"Light shifts and polarisabilities","text":"Builds the per-state near-resonant channel lists over basis at the static flux density B: for every state, one entry per Zeeman component connecting it to the other manifold of each relevant level pair — the electric-quadrupole pairs within the basis, plus the reference pair of a RelativeFrequency laser (of either rank). Channel positions are exact at-field offsets from the pair's zero-field interval; for the reference pair that is the very reference the laser offset is measured against, for the others the reference cancels out of the driven-mode differences it is used in.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.resonant_channels"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/47-polarisability/#Levels.resonant_state_shift-NTuple{8, Any}","page":"Light shifts and polarisabilities","text":"Sums the signed near-resonant shift of the basis state index over its channels to the other fine-structure manifold, for a laser at δ from the channels' reference and the given geometric weights (w_pol indexed q + 2 for rank 1, w_quad indexed q + 3 for rank 2); exclude drops the channel to one partner state (the resonantly driven component of driven_light_shift).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.resonant_state_shift"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/47-polarisability/#Levels.scalar_polarisability-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Light shifts and polarisabilities","text":"scalar_polarisability(species, level, laser)\n\nReturns the dynamic scalar polarisability α_0(ω) of the given level at the given laser wavelength or angular frequency.\n\nTogether with vector_polarisability and tensor_polarisability, this decomposes the polarisability of the sublevel m for polarisation ε as\n\nα = α_0 + 𝒜 fracm2j α_1 + frac3 ε_0^2 - 12 frac3 m^2 - j(j+1)j(2j-1) α_2,\n\nwhere 𝒜 = ε_-1^2 - ε_+1^2 is the degree of circular polarisation about the quantisation axis and ε_0 = ε  ẑ. light_shift evaluates the equivalent sum directly, without going through this decomposition.\n\nFor a hyperfine F level (with j replaced by F in the decomposition), the components are extracted from the per-channel polarisabilities of the stretched state; cf. Levels.state_polarisabilities for the approximations involved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.scalar_polarisability"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/47-polarisability/#Levels.shift_at_intensity-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Light shifts and polarisabilities","text":"Converts a polarisability into the corresponding shift at the given intensity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.shift_at_intensity"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/47-polarisability/#Levels.state_polarisabilities-Tuple{Any, StateSpec, Any}","page":"Light shifts and polarisabilities","text":"Returns the polarisability of state for each of the three Δm channels separately, as a length-3 vector indexed by q + 2.\n\nFor hyperfine states of a HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, the explicit channels are summed over the hyperfine levels of each intermediate fine-structure level, with the F-basis angular factors (mathrmCG  β, cf. Levels.hyperfine_reduction) and the intermediate-state hyperfine splittings resolved in the detunings (levels without known hyperfine constants keep degenerate F levels at their centroid). The hyperfine-resolved detunings are what gives e.g. the ⁴³Ca⁺ S_12 F levels their small tensor polarisability ( A_PΔ), which the fine-structure J = 12 level lacks; with the splittings zeroed, the result collapses exactly to the standard 6-j re-projection of the fine-structure polarisability. The tensor part of the lumped static remainder is re-projected with the expectation value of the J-basis tensor operator in the coupled state (diagonal-in-F approximation; the F-off-diagonal elements are second order in the hyperfine mixing).\n\nThe exclude keyword names one intermediate fine-structure level whose co-rotating term is left out of the sum — used when that channel is near-resonant and handled Zeeman-resolved by the channel machinery of LightShiftCoefficients instead; its smooth counter-rotating term stays in the background. The excluded channel must be explicit in the LevelPolarisability data, not lumped into the static remainder, which cannot be split.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.state_polarisabilities"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/47-polarisability/#Levels.state_polarisability-Tuple{LightShiftCoefficients, Integer, Any}","page":"Light shifts and polarisabilities","text":"Returns the polarisability of the basis state with the given index for the polarisation_weights w.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.state_polarisability"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/47-polarisability/#Levels.stretched_channels-Tuple{HyperfineOneElectronSpecies, HyperfineNumberSpec, Any}","page":"Light shifts and polarisabilities","text":"Returns the per-channel polarisabilities of the stretched state m = F of a hyperfine level, from which the α_0/α_1/α_2 decomposition is extracted (α_σ^- = α_0 - α_12 - α_22, α_π = α_0 + α_2, α_σ^+ = α_0 + α_12 - α_22 there, as the tensor angular factor is unity at m = F).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.stretched_channels"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/47-polarisability/#Levels.tensor_polarisability-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Light shifts and polarisabilities","text":"tensor_polarisability(species, level, laser)\n\nReturns the dynamic tensor polarisability α_2(ω) of the given level at the given laser wavelength or angular frequency (see scalar_polarisability for the convention).\n\nZero for j  12, which has no oriented sublevels to distinguish.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.tensor_polarisability"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/47-polarisability/#Levels.validate_background_detunings-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"Light shifts and polarisabilities","text":"Raises an error if the laser photon energy ħω falls within Levels.BACKGROUND_GUARD_WINDOW of an explicit electric-dipole channel of any of the given levels, except the excluded (reference-pair) partner.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.validate_background_detunings"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/47-polarisability/#Levels.validate_parts-Tuple{Any}","page":"Light shifts and polarisabilities","text":"Raises an error unless parts is a valid part selector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.validate_parts"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/47-polarisability/#Levels.validate_reference-Tuple{Any, RelativeFrequency}","page":"Light shifts and polarisabilities","text":"Validates that the reference pair of a RelativeFrequency laser is a transition the channel machinery can describe, returning its ordered fine-structure levels and multipole rank.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.validate_reference"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/47-polarisability/#Levels.validate_shift_field-Tuple{Any}","page":"Light shifts and polarisabilities","text":"Raises an error unless B is a sensible signed scalar flux density for the near-resonant channel construction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.validate_shift_field"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/47-polarisability/#Levels.vector_polarisability-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Light shifts and polarisabilities","text":"vector_polarisability(species, level, laser)\n\nReturns the dynamic vector polarisability α_1(ω) of the given level at the given laser wavelength or angular frequency (see scalar_polarisability for the convention).\n\nThe vector polarisability vanishes in the static limit, so the far-detuned remainder lumped into LevelPolarisability.static_scalar contributes nothing to it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Levels.vector_polarisability"}]
}
